+# TOSEM submission and formatting notes
+
+Venue sources were checked in the preceding revision on 3 October 2026; artifact counts below are updated for the 4 October character-domain correction. This is an author handoff, not confirmation that a submission has occurred or that a paper will be accepted.
+
+## Length: distinguish the track rule from a universal limit
+
+The indexed text of TOSEM's official **Fast-Impact Track Papers** page says that the editorial staff upgrades journal-first submissions with no more than **45 pages of text, excluding the bibliography**, to the fast-impact track. The official Survey Papers page separately says papers of any length are accepted and describes the same 45-page fast-review threshold. The survey-page wording should not be silently promoted to an unconditional rule for every regular-research submission category.
+
+The general author-guidelines and track pages returned access errors when opened directly in this environment; official indexed excerpts and the published SIGSOFT editorial were accessible. Accordingly, this handoff does not assert that all ordinary research submissions have no upper bound, nor that this project qualifies as journal-first. The live submission form and the paper's actual prior-publication history control that choice.
+
+The delivered manuscript is **41 total single-column review-format pages** (references start on page 38). Even counting the bibliography, it is below the 45-page text threshold. The revised manuscript develops matching and composition arguments, joint snapshot freshness, issuer-bridge checks, bounded wildcard validation, measured local recomputation costs, and the complete character-support argument with its explicit validation boundaries. No blank pages or layout inflation were used.
+
+Sources:
+- ACM TOSEM, Fast-Impact Track Papers: https://dl.acm.org/journal/tosem/fastimpacttrackpapers
+- ACM TOSEM, Survey Papers: https://dl.acm.org/journal/tosem/survey-papers
+- ACM TOSEM, Author Guidelines: https://dl.acm.org/journal/tosem/author-guidelines
+- SIGSOFT editorial, Why Publish in ACM TOSEM in the Twenties? (2022): https://sigsoft.medium.com/why-publish-in-acm-tosem-in-the-twenties-8e7326f264ab
+
+## Review template and production
+
+ACM's author instructions require the single-column `manuscript` option for review. The source uses `\documentclass[manuscript,screen,review]{acmart}` and `\acmJournal{TOSEM}`, with ordinary template fonts/margins and editable TikZ figures with `\Description` text. It does not retain a conference `sigconf` layout.
+
+The delivered PDF was compiled with the installed, unmodified `acmart` v2.12 (2024-12-28). The current CTAN listing reports v2.20 (2026-08-16). The official repository's v2.20 tag and four source-file blob identities were also checked. No inference about template freshness is made from the installed class. Attempts to download the current class failed because external downloads/DNS were unavailable in the execution environment. Therefore the PDF is a verified build of the included manuscript source using the installed class, **not a claimed test of the latest class or a TAPS production certification**. Authors should compile the same source with the current official submission template before an actual upload and inspect any pagination changes. No font files or an edited class are bundled.
+
+Publisher-assigned DOI, volume, issue, article number, publication date and rights text are not invented. The review copy suppresses the publication-reference block and copyright-permission footnote; the installed class consequently emits its standard reference-block warning, recorded in the build notes. There are no unresolved citations, undefined references or overfull text boxes in the checked build. Post-acceptance production metadata must be supplied by ACM, not guessed now. The ordinary footer “Manuscript submitted to ACM” is class text, not a claim that an upload was made.
+
+Sources:
+- ACM, Submissions: https://www.acm.org/publications/authors/submissions
+- ACM, LaTeX best practices: https://www.acm.org/binaries/content/assets/publications/taps/latex-best_practices-06-may-2020.pdf
+- CTAN package listing: https://ctan.org/pkg/acmart
+
+## Author-controlled declarations before submission
+
+The supplied author order and emails are preserved. Xunzhu Tang's University of Luxembourg affiliation is provisional, with no corporate affiliation added. Author approval, contribution statements, current affiliations, the corresponding-author choice, ORCIDs, funding, conflicts and public redistribution rights have not been verified by this workflow. They must not be auto-filled as confirmed facts.
+
+Confirm whether the original FSE-named package was merely a draft, was submitted, or was published. Disclose relevant overlap and avoid concurrent submission as required by the venue; a filename does not establish publication history or journal-first eligibility. Confirm any anonymity option in the live submission form. No manuscript was submitted or uploaded publicly here.
+
+AI assistance in this revision was substantial: source consistency inspection, implementation edits, synthetic test construction, local execution, analysis, figure authoring and manuscript drafting. The human authors must check the current ACM disclosure policy, review and take responsibility for all content, and make an accurate disclosure; this is not just grammar polishing. A specific disclosure is included in the manuscript acknowledgments. This does not assert that the human authors have approved its wording or independently checked every generated contribution.
+
+## Evidence and licensing boundaries
+
+Local evaluation did not use real cloud accounts, live tokens, cloud SDK calls, remote workflow execution, or maintainer contact. The recorded public run identifiers are historical metadata, not reproduced runs. Frozen public snippets/patches retain attribution. Their supplied hashes prove local correspondence only, not authenticated remote provenance. Authors remain responsible for redistribution permissions and repository licenses before public artifact release. The package adds no blanket license that purports to relicense third-party material.
+
+## Explicit current-template bootstrap
+
+`make template-current` from the artifact directory is an **optional networked operation**, separate from `make reproduce`. It downloads only the four pinned v2.20 class/style/license sources, checks their exact size and Git blob identity, generates the unmodified official class, and rebuilds. It never downloads font files. The recorded attempt in this environment failed at DNS resolution; `logs/template-install-attempt.log` preserves the failure. This command has not been reported as a successful v2.20 compilation.
+
+`paper/generated/build_environment.json` records the class actually selected by TeX, its SHA-256, the TeX/BibTeX versions, and the resulting PDF hash. The build discovers `bibtex`, `bibtex.original`, or `bibtex8` in that order; it does not replace the ACM bibliography style to work around a missing executable.
