+\begin{abstract}
+A change to a CI/CD trust policy can remove unintended access while also excluding a required release identity. Reviewing the policy alone is insufficient when the issuer's claims, repository governance, and the intended release contract evolve separately. We present \FF{}, a specification-guided method for reviewing supplied trust-change snapshots. Its contract separates an upper bound on admissible identities from explicit positive regression identities. The method returns \Pass{} only when supported, fresh, internally consistent inputs establish both bounds; it returns \Fail{} for supported conformance violations and \Unknown{} when a premise is unavailable or inconsistent. A set-theoretic account characterizes the observations that are safe for every mintable state they represent and explains when lost distinctions prevent a policy-only repair. The existing prototype combines a restricted regular-language policy analyzer, scalar positive checks, dependency tracking, and replayable review receipts. Receipts bind the complete two-sided judgment without treating content hashes as authentication or prior results as a cache. Offline evaluation includes \TestCount{} regression tests, \ProjectionCount{} bounded projection checks, \FiniteCount{} finite two-sided decisions, \StrictCount{} literal-policy cases and \GlobStrictCount{} finitely bounded glob cases through the strict gate, and \MatcherCount{} bounded matcher comparisons. A source-backed study distinguishes \PublicCommits{} commits, \PublicRoles{} role contracts, and \PublicConfigurations{} evaluated configurations; a separate audit verifies \SourceCount{} frozen source excerpts but performs no full-source extraction. These results support implementation consistency and the analysis of explicitly supplied models, not population prevalence, live cloud enforcement, user productivity, or comparative tool superiority.
