+\section{Introduction}
+\label{sec:intro}
+A release pipeline is both a software workflow and an authorization client. In a federated setup, an issuer supplies a signed identity token, and a cloud role evaluates selected claims to decide whether to admit that identity. OpenID Connect and JSON Web Token specifications define the identity and token mechanisms; they do not determine which release contexts a particular project intends to authorize~\cite{openid,jwt,jws}. GitHub's guidance for OpenID Connect with AWS accordingly connects trust-policy conditions to repository and workflow context~\cite{github-oidc,github-aws}. The engineering difficulty is that these contexts are maintained in different artifacts. A pull request may change a trust policy while the release branches, environment restrictions, or issuer profile remain outside the textual diff.
+
+Consider a project that allows releases from \code{main} and \code{release}, but requires the \code{main} deployment to remain available. Replacing a repository-wide subject pattern with an exact \code{release} subject reduces the set of admitted identities. This change can satisfy a least-privilege upper bound while breaking the project's required release path. Conversely, preserving a known successful identity says nothing about whether unrelated branches remain admitted. These are separate review obligations. Neither textual narrowing nor a positive example alone establishes the conjunction.
+
+A second difficulty is information loss. A policy observes claims rather than the full workflow state. In the legacy subject profile modeled by \FF{}, an environment subject identifies an environment but does not itself identify the branch that selected it. If both an intended and an unintended branch can reach the same environment subject under the supplied governance model, accepting that subject admits both. Restricting the subject string cannot recover a distinction that the projection has removed. An analysis must either establish a governance premise that excludes the unintended state, use a supported richer observation, or decline a positive judgment. Inferring intent from the existing trust policy would hide this problem by making the implementation its own specification.
+
+\FF{} addresses these difficulties as a \emph{change-review} problem. A review packet contains a separately declared release contract, an issuer profile, workflow and governance snapshots, and a policy snapshot. The contract provides an upper bound on permitted observations and a finite set of required positive identities. The policy is analyzed against both. Explicit freshness and consistency checks determine whether the packet supports a semantic judgment at all. A result is thus relative to supplied inputs and the implemented profile, rather than a claim about an unobserved cloud deployment.
+
+The three outcomes have distinct meanings. \Pass{} states that both conformance obligations hold under supported, fresh, internally consistent premises. \Fail{} states that such premises expose over-admission or the loss of a valid required identity. \Unknown{} states that a premise needed for the judgment is missing, stale, unsupported, or contradictory. The priority matters: an invalid required identity cannot be converted into a supported regression finding merely because some other part of the policy also over-admits. The report can retain that diagnostic, but the overall judgment remains \Unknown{}.
+
+The method retains the existing \FF{} implementation rather than replacing it with a new authorization engine. Its regular-language core reasons about a restricted GitHub-to-AWS subject/audience fragment. A strict wrapper validates the review packet and enforces profile boundaries. Scalar checks supply the positive obligation, while dependency hashes and replay connect the result to the reviewed inputs. The journal treatment makes these components' relationships explicit and corrects inconsistencies between the original integration, study adapters, and reporting scripts.
+
+The contribution is the combination of four review mechanisms with explicit boundaries. First, a two-sided contract distinguishes permitted scope from mandatory regression identities and assigns unsupported judgments a separate outcome. Second, an observation-level semantics identifies the greatest safe set of mintable observations, including the case where intended and unintended states share a claim tuple. Third, a snapshot-relative receipt binds the full review judgment and reruns it instead of treating a prior safety certificate as a reusable deployment authorization. Fourth, an executable evidence protocol separates finite-model checks, strict-gate checks, frozen source cases, and source-extraction availability. The mathematical set identities are elementary; the contribution is their use to make cross-artifact review obligations and implementation evidence auditable, not a new general theorem about authorization.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Review questions that must not be substituted for one another. ``Established'' always refers to the supplied model and supported fragment.}
+\label{tab:questions}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.24\linewidth}XX@{}}
+\toprule
+Question & Required evidence & Insufficient substitute \\
+\midrule
+Does the policy over-admit? & Effective accepted tuples compared with a separate upper bound & The diff appears narrower \\
+Does a release identity survive? & A valid required tuple is mintable, intended, and accepted & No upper-bound violation was found \\
+Can the policy express intent? & Observation classes do not mix required intended states with unintended states & An intended state has that subject \\
+Can an old result be reused? & Current dependencies, time, semantics, and full replay agree & A content hash or old safety certificate exists \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+Our evaluation is deliberately narrower than a deployment study. It includes \TestCount{} unit and regression tests, \ProjectionCount{} bounded checks of the state-to-observation relationship, \FiniteCount{} finite two-sided decisions, \StrictCount{} literal-policy inputs and \GlobStrictCount{} finitely bounded glob inputs through the complete strict gate, and separate matcher/issuer-membership comparisons. A small local-cost experiment records full recomputation rather than presuming a cache advantage. These checks use directly specified benign inputs and local execution. The public-change study reconstructs \PublicConfigurations{} configurations from \PublicCommits{} supplied commit snapshots while preserving role and variable-branch distinctions. The source-frontier audit checks \SourceCount{} frozen excerpts but has no complete source files on which to measure extraction accuracy. We report these units separately rather than combining them into an inflated benchmark size.
+
+The results support a concrete engineering conclusion: the reviewed implementation can be exercised consistently against its declared contracts, and several tempting summaries of the original evidence are not justified. They do not establish the frequency of trust failures in a population, the correctness of a live issuer, the enforcement of repository settings, or the superiority of \FF{} over other tools. Those boundaries are part of the method because the review result itself must not claim more than its premises support.
