+\section{Problem Setting and Running Example}
+\label{sec:setting}
+\subsection{Identity tokens, policy observations, and release intent}
+JWT separates a token's claims from its cryptographic representation, and JWS specifies signed representations~\cite{jwt,jws}. Successful signature verification is not, by itself, a project-specific authorization policy. Best-current-practice guidance also distinguishes token validation requirements such as the intended audience and acceptable algorithms~\cite{jwt-bcp}. \FF{} does not validate live tokens, fetch keys, or perform an authentication protocol. It reasons about the claim tuples described by a supplied issuer model and policy.
+
+The supported strict profile concerns a single GitHub OIDC provider and a role trust policy with the action \code{sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity}. Its observed coordinates are the scalar subject and audience. A subject in the modeled legacy family can identify a branch, tag, pull-request context, or environment. This is a \emph{versioned implementation profile}, not a declaration that every current repository emits that format. A supplied custom or immutable subject profile is not silently translated into the legacy family. Public evidence involving such profiles is handled by a separately identified finite study adapter, not represented as a successful strict-gate run.
+
+IAM combines policy statements and condition operators according to defined evaluation rules, including the effect of explicit denial~\cite{iam-logic,iam-operators}. \FF{} implements only a trust-policy slice of this behavior. It does not model service control policies, every identity or resource policy, token signature validation, the role's permissions after assumption, or session behavior. Its use of the word ``admitted'' denotes membership in the effective language of the supported supplied trust policy. A local \Pass{} does not mean that an AWS request would succeed.
+
+The release contract is an additional project artifact. It specifies a repository and role, intended branch/tag/environment contexts, accepted audiences, and optional required identity tuples. The upper bound answers which identities \emph{may} be accepted. Required identities answer which exact positive examples \emph{must not be excluded by the trust policy}. These declarations are not learned from the policy under review. Allowing the policy to generate its own upper bound would make arbitrary broadening appear conformant; allowing it to generate its own positive set would make denial of all identities appear acceptable.
+
+\subsection{A benign two-sided regression}
+Let the permitted release contexts for \code{acme/api} be \code{main} and \code{release}. Both use audience \code{sts.amazonaws.com}. Let $m$ and $r$ denote the corresponding subject/audience tuples, and let $d$ denote a \code{dev} tuple. The upper bound is $I=\{m,r\}$, while the explicit regression requirement is $Q=\{m\}$. All three tuples are mintable in the example model. Table~\ref{tab:running} shows why both obligations are needed.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{A fully specified local example. $I=\{m,r\}$ and $Q=\{m\}$ remain fixed. These are constructed cases, not observations from a cloud account.}
+\label{tab:running}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.21\linewidth}p{.16\linewidth}p{.14\linewidth}X@{}}
+\toprule
+Effective policy & Upper bound & Required identity & Judgment and explanation \\
+\midrule
+$\{m,r,d\}$ & Violated & Preserved & \Fail{}: an unintended identity is admitted \\
+$\{m,r\}$ & Satisfied & Preserved & \Pass{}: both obligations hold \\
+$\{r\}$ & Satisfied & Missing & \Fail{}: narrowing removed the required identity \\
+$\varnothing$ & Satisfied & Missing & \Fail{} in the set model; a supported Allow/Deny construction can realize it \\
+$\{m\}$ with stale input & Not established & Not established & \Unknown{}: the freshness premise fails \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+The last but one row is a useful specification test. A safety-only containment property accepts the empty policy vacuously. Positive regression identities prevent that particular failure mode without requiring the policy to admit every identity in the upper bound. The distinction is intentional: permitted scope is often broader than the release paths selected as mandatory regression checks. A project that needs preservation of an entire regular language would need an additional language-level lower-bound obligation; the current positive mechanism is finite.
+
+The strict wrapper requires at least one supported Allow statement. Therefore a syntactically deny-only document is outside its profile even when a more general IAM interpreter could derive a denial. The empty effective set in the running example can instead be expressed with a supported Allow and an equal supported Deny. We retain this distinction between set-model expressiveness and concrete syntax so that a mathematical example does not falsely imply support for every equivalent source form.
+
+\subsection{Why an environment subject can lose a branch distinction}
+Suppose a release intent permits the production environment only from \code{main}. In the modeled legacy profile, two full states can share the same environment subject: one on \code{main}, the other on \code{dev}. If both are mintable under the supplied governance snapshot, the policy sees the same tuple for both. Selecting the environment subject admits the unintended state as well. An environment name is consequently not a substitute for an established branch restriction.
+
+GitHub exposes environment configuration and deployment protection concepts~\cite{github-environments}. \FF{} does not fetch or authenticate those settings. Instead, the strict packet must explicitly supply the relevant environment-rule premises, including reviewed restrictions, a disabled bypass declaration, allowed refs, and a source reference. The checker validates consistency with the contract. It does not prove that the repository enforces the declaration. A stale or incomplete declaration cannot be repaired by inventing a branch claim.
+
+\input{figures/collision}
+
+\subsection{Review actors and trust assumptions}
+The intended actors are a change author, a contract reviewer, and a local review runner. The author proposes changes to one or more snapshots. The reviewer supplies or approves the release contract. The runner evaluates the packet at a stated time and records the result. The packet requires distinct declared author and reviewer strings and an approved contract digest. These are structural checks on the supplied record, not authentication of the people or their authority.
+
+Our adversarial boundary is correspondingly limited. The method is intended to expose policy/contract inconsistencies and unsupported premises in the input. It does not withstand a malicious party that can invent every snapshot, author every declaration, alter the trusted runner, and replace the final result without an independently authenticated review process. Content hashes detect disagreement between bytes and a recorded digest; they do not prove that those bytes came from GitHub or AWS. A timestamp records an asserted observation time, not a cryptographically verified capture event.
+
+This distinction makes the result usable without overloading it. A trusted organizational process could authenticate snapshot capture and protect the contract review independently. The local checker could then be one component in that process. Such integration is a deployment responsibility and was not performed in this study. The artifact explicitly records that source authenticity, reviewer authentication, workflow reachability, runtime validation, and deployment authorization have not been established.
