+\section{Review Packets and Decision Semantics}
+\label{sec:contract}
+\subsection{Packet structure and independent specification}
+A strict review packet has five semantic inputs: the release contract and four snapshots covering the issuer, workflow, governance, and trust policy. Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows how these inputs enter the existing implementation. The contract uses schema \code{fedfence-intent-v1}. It identifies one exact repository and target role. Its intent fields list literal branches, tags, environments, permitted audiences, and whether the pull-request context is permitted. The profile deliberately rejects interpolation and ambiguous patterns in these fields. At least one intended identity form and one audience must be declared.
+
+The contract may include \code{required\_tokens}. Each record contains nonempty string values for \code{sub} and \code{aud} and a nonempty explanation. Duplicate tuples are rejected. These records are not token credentials and contain no signature or bearer secret. They are positive test obligations over the modeled identity relation. A tuple must first be valid under the issuer model and inside the declared intent. Only then does a policy rejection constitute a missing-required-identity failure.
+
+The contract's approved digest covers its body excluding the review metadata itself. A changed intent, required tuple, reason, repository scope, role, author, or freshness policy changes the approved content and requires a matching review record. This prevents accidental acceptance of an unchanged approval after editing the contract. It does not prevent a party with access to the packet from editing both fields. The review record therefore supports change detection, not non-repudiation.
+
+\input{figures/architecture}
+
+Each snapshot supplies a body, a digest of that body, a source description, and an observation timestamp. The packet validates the repository or role scope where applicable. Source descriptions are included in the dependency identity even when the underlying body is unchanged. A policy copied from another role or governance copied from another repository must not be accepted merely because its syntax is valid. Workflow content is bound into the packet, but the current implementation does not derive a reachable-state model from a workflow program. This conservative dependency does not amount to workflow analysis.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Input responsibilities and limits of the strict packet. A checked declaration is not an authenticated external fact.}
+\label{tab:packet}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.16\linewidth}XX@{}}
+\toprule
+Input & Checked responsibility & Deliberately unestablished fact \\
+\midrule
+Contract & Schema, literal intent, distinct declared reviewer, approval digest, positive-record shape & Actual reviewer identity or organizational authority \\
+Issuer & Repository scope and supported legacy subject/audience profile & Current issuer behavior or live token authenticity \\
+Workflow & Scope, body digest, source metadata, timestamp & Reachability, execution semantics, or successful release \\
+Governance & Scope and required environment restrictions relative to intent & Enforcement of those restrictions in a repository \\
+Policy & Role scope, digest, timestamp, supported statement/operator fragment & The complete effective cloud authorization decision \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+\subsection{Freshness is a premise, not a performance optimization}
+The freshness bound is an integer from one second to 31 days. For each of the four snapshots, with supplied observation time $t_s$, review time $t$, and bound $\Delta$, validity requires
+\begin{equation}
+0\leq t-t_s\leq\Delta.
+\label{eq:freshness}
+\end{equation}
+Timestamps must have an explicit time zone and are normalized to UTC. Future-dated, missing, unparsable, or expired observations invalidate the packet. The inequality is inclusive at the declared boundary, which is covered by a regression test. The bound is selected by the contract, not inferred from how recently a CI job happened to run.
+
+A body hash can remain unchanged while its evidence expires. Conversely, refreshing an asserted timestamp does not independently refresh the source: capture authenticity remains outside the checker. Deterministic experiments pass an explicit historical \code{--now}; operational review should omit that test override and use the actual check time. The reproduction harness makes this difference visible rather than presenting historical fixture freshness as current deployment evidence.
+
+The dependency vector includes hashes for the contract and snapshots as well as the current implementation. The previous result is used only to report changed dependencies. The checker does not skip validation when the vector appears unchanged. In particular, the clock is a changing premise even when all input files are byte-identical. A design that memoized \Pass{} solely by file hash would violate Equation~\ref{eq:freshness} after expiration.
+
+\subsection{Three outcomes and diagnostic precedence}
+Let $V$ denote the validity of all required premises, $O$ the existence of a supported upper-bound violation, and $L$ the loss of a valid required identity. The final decision rule is
+\begin{equation}
+\operatorname{decide}(V,O,L)=
+\begin{cases}
+\Unknown,&\neg V,\\
+\Fail,&V\land(O\lor L),\\
+\Pass,&V\land\neg O\land\neg L.
+\end{cases}
+\label{eq:decision}
+\end{equation}
+An analysis that cannot complete within its resource budget also lacks the premise needed for a positive semantic result. The command-line timeout must be a finite positive number; invalid budgets do not fall through to an ordinary policy judgment. A backend error, unsupported construct, or replay disagreement similarly cannot be reclassified as safe.
+
+\input{figures/decision}
+
+The precedence does not require hiding useful diagnostics. For example, the policy may admit a \code{dev} tuple, while a required \code{release} tuple is outside the declared upper bound. The report can display the over-admission diagnostic and the inconsistent positive declaration together. Its top-level outcome is nevertheless \Unknown{} because the packet has not provided a coherent specification for the full judgment. This is a logical status distinction, not a severity ranking. In a blocking CI integration both \Fail{} and \Unknown{} produce nonzero exit codes, but they call for different repairs.
+
+A \Fail{} invites a policy or explicitly reviewed contract change. An \Unknown{} invites resolution of the missing premise: supplying a supported profile, correcting the contract, obtaining an appropriately fresh snapshot, resolving an input error, or increasing a justified local analysis budget. Repeatedly narrowing the policy is not a generic remedy for \Unknown{}. For an observation collision, additional narrowing can eliminate every useful identity without making the intended distinction expressible.
+
+\subsection{Comparing changes without inferring intent from the diff}
+The comparison command reviews complete before and after packets separately. For an unchanged contract, a supported \Pass{} to \Fail{} transition is a confirmed regression relative to that contract. The report distinguishes new upper-bound violations from required-identity loss. A previously failing policy may improve without reaching \Pass{}, and an \Unknown{} result does not establish either a repair or a regression. The outcomes are not points on a single numerical scale.
+
+A contract change changes the question. For example, approving an additional release branch may make a previously excessive policy conformant, but that is not evidence that the policy alone was repaired. The comparison therefore exposes the changed contract dependency and treats the transition as review of a new specification. Likewise, changing the issuer or governance model can change which tuples are mintable. The result must identify the changed premise rather than attributing every outcome change to the policy text.
+
+These rules preserve the distinction between review and automatic deployment. The artifact produces machine-readable JSON and human-oriented text, SARIF, or GitHub annotations. Their presentation does not strengthen the underlying evidence. Exit codes are 0 for \Pass{}, 1 for \Fail{}, and 2 for \Unknown{}. The explicit deployment-authorization flag remains false in this local workflow.
