+\section{State-to-Observation Semantics}
+\label{sec:semantics}
+\subsection{Domains, projections, and the effective policy}
+Let $W$ be a set of workflow states. A state can contain distinctions absent from the policy, such as a branch associated with an environment. Let $M\subseteq W$ be the states declared mintable by the supplied issuer and governance model. Let $\Phi\subseteq W$ be the release intent and put $R=M\cap\Phi$. Restricting to $M$ \emph{before} taking images matters: states excluded by the supplied governance premise must not create spurious collisions, while mintable unintended states must not disappear merely because they are absent from the contract.
+
+Let $\pi:W\rightarrow Y$ project a state to the complete observation used by the modeled policy. In the strict profile, $Y$ consists of subject/audience tuples. Let $A\subseteq Y$ and $D\subseteq Y$ be the unions of matching Allow and Deny observations, respectively, and let $E=A\setminus D$ be the effective policy. Define
+\begin{equation}
+G=\pi(M),\qquad
+\Adm_M(E)=\{w\in M\mid\pi(w)\in E\}.
+\label{eq:admission}
+\end{equation}
+State safety is $\Adm_M(E)\subseteq R$. The policy may contain observations that are not in $G$; they do not admit a state in the supplied model. Such observations can still motivate diagnostic questions about model completeness, but they are not state-safety counterexamples without a mintability premise.
+
+An observation class is $[y]_M=\{w\in M\mid\pi(w)=y\}$. A class can be entirely intended, entirely unintended, or mixed. The naive image $\pi(R)$ selects every observation with \emph{some} intended preimage. Safety requires a universal condition over its mintable preimages instead.
+
+\begin{definition}[Must-safe observations]
+For a supplied mintable domain, intent, and projection, define
+\begin{equation}
+H=\Must(M,\Phi,\pi)
+ =G\setminus\pi(M\setminus R)
+ =\{y\in G\mid [y]_M\subseteq R\}.
+\label{eq:must}
+\end{equation}
+\end{definition}
+The restriction $y\in G$ excludes empty observation classes. Thus $H$ is the greatest set of \emph{mintable} observations all of whose represented states are intended. It is not a set of observations merely witnessed by an intended state.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Observation containment exactly characterizes supplied-state safety]
+\label{prop:safety}
+For any $E\subseteq Y$,
+\begin{equation}
+\Adm_M(E)\subseteq R
+\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+G\cap E\subseteq H.
+\label{eq:equivalence}
+\end{equation}
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Assume state safety. If $y\in G\cap E$, every $w\in[y]_M$ is admitted by definition and hence belongs to $R$. Therefore $y\in H$. Conversely, assume observation containment and take any admitted state $w$. Then $\pi(w)\in G\cap E\subseteq H$. The definition of $H$ places every mintable preimage of $\pi(w)$, including $w$, in $R$.
+\end{proof}
+
+This equivalence is a statement about the supplied mathematical relation. It is not a proof that a parser, NFA library, source normalizer, or cloud provider implements that relation. We keep the distinction explicit because the executable validation checks the former components only on bounded or restricted inputs.
+
+\subsection{When the simpler intended image is sufficient}
+\begin{definition}[Saturation]
+Intent is saturated over $M$ under $\pi$ when no observation class is mixed, equivalently
+\begin{equation}
+\pi(R)\cap\pi(M\setminus R)=\varnothing.
+\label{eq:saturation}
+\end{equation}
+\end{definition}
+Under saturation, $H=\pi(R)$, and the upper-bound check reduces to $G\cap E\subseteq\pi(R)$. Without saturation this reduction is unsound. To see this, take $M=\{w_m,w_d\}$, $R=\{w_m\}$, and $\pi(w_m)=\pi(w_d)=y$. Then $\pi(R)=\{y\}$ but $H=\varnothing$. The policy $E=\{y\}$ satisfies the naive containment and nevertheless admits $w_d$.
+
+The bounded projection audit enumerates exactly this distinction. Its stored counterexample includes two admitted states that share an observation, only one of which is intended. The audit does not merely count successful cases; it preserves the false existential-lifting example so that future refactoring cannot quietly replace a universal premise with a witness premise.
+
+Saturation is useful for understanding a policy-only repair. If a required intended state shares all policy-visible coordinates with an unintended mintable state, no predicate on those coordinates can admit the former and exclude the latter. This is not a limitation of a particular solver. It follows from the input/output behavior of a predicate applied to the same tuple. A remedy must change the admitted state domain, the observation, or the requirement. Which remedy is operationally available is a separate provider and governance question.
+
+\subsection{Positive obligations and realizability}
+Let $Q\subseteq Y$ be a finite set of required observations. The two semantic obligations are
+\begin{equation}
+G\cap E\subseteq H
+\qquad\text{and}\qquad
+Q\subseteq G\cap E.
+\label{eq:two-sided}
+\end{equation}
+A coherent requirement must have $Q\subseteq H$. In the strict implementation, intent compilation and the environment-governance checks establish the profile-relative upper bound, and each required tuple is checked for mintability and membership in that bound. The finite normalized study instead takes an explicitly supplied issuer relation and intent relation. Neither path may silently enlarge intent in response to a required tuple that falls outside it.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Two-sided realizability over observations]
+\label{prop:realizable}
+There exists an observation predicate $E\subseteq Y$ satisfying Equation~\ref{eq:two-sided} if and only if $Q\subseteq H$. When the condition holds, $E=Q$ is one solution, and $H$ is the greatest mintable safe effective set.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+If a solution exists, $Q\subseteq G\cap E\subseteq H$. Conversely, if $Q\subseteq H$, then $Q\subseteq G$ and $E=Q$ satisfies both inclusions. Every safe predicate's mintable observations are contained in $H$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:safety}, while accepting $H$ itself is safe.
+\end{proof}
+
+Realizability here ranges over arbitrary predicates on $Y$. It does not prove that a provider offers a condition key for every coordinate or a source language can conveniently express the resulting predicate. The proposition supplies a necessary semantic test and an abstract witness. Concrete policy support still passes through the strict fragment check. If the supplied $Q$ is empty, the lower-bound obligation is vacuous; the packet reports zero required identities rather than claiming availability testing occurred.
+
+\subsection{Tuple relations and explicit denial}
+A statement that allows subjects in $S_i$ with audiences in $U_i$ denotes the rectangle $S_i\times U_i$. Its subject and audience conditions must hold together. Multiple Allow statements and multiple Deny statements yield
+\begin{equation}
+E=\left(\bigcup_i S_i^+\times U_i^+\right)
+  \setminus\left(\bigcup_j S_j^-\times U_j^-\right).
+\label{eq:tuple-policy}
+\end{equation}
+Projecting or subtracting coordinates separately does not generally preserve this relation. For example, allowing all four pairs from $\{s_1,s_2\}\times\{a_1,a_2\}$ and denying only $(s_1,a_1)$ leaves three admitted pairs. Subtracting the denied subject from all subjects incorrectly removes $(s_1,a_2)$; subtracting the denied audience incorrectly removes $(s_2,a_1)$. Conversely, losing statement correlations can add pairs never allowed together.
+
+\input{figures/tuples}
+
+The regular-language backend retains the relevant conjunctions when searching for witnesses. The scalar positive checker evaluates each complete statement against the same tuple and computes matching Allow minus matching Deny. The literal-policy differential experiment tests these two implementations against direct tuple-set subtraction, rather than comparing subject-only summaries. This check is especially important because a required identity can survive a subject restriction yet be excluded by an audience-specific denial.
