+\subsection{How changes in premises affect review obligations}
+\label{sec:relational}
+A change can alter $M$, $\Phi$, $\pi$, or $E$. The following properties isolate their effects. They are elementary consequences of the definitions, included to guide review explanations and bounded regression checks. They are not claims that arbitrary changes to a real repository satisfy the stated premises.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Restricting mintability]
+\label{prop:restriction}
+For fixed $\Phi$ and $\pi$, if $M'\subseteq M$, then
+\begin{equation}
+\Must(M,\Phi,\pi)\cap\pi(M')\subseteq\Must(M',\Phi,\pi).
+\end{equation}
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+A surviving observation whose entire old mintable class was intended cannot acquire an unintended preimage by deleting states. Its new nonempty class is a subset of the old one and remains intended.
+\end{proof}
+A stronger governance restriction can therefore remove an ambiguous unintended preimage and make an observation safe. It can also remove a required observation from $G$. Consequently it is not sufficient to carry forward an old safety result after restricting $M$; the positive obligation must be rechecked. ``More restrictive governance'' is not synonymous with ``release compatibility preserved.''
+
+\begin{proposition}[Expanding intent]
+\label{prop:intent}
+For fixed $M$ and $\pi$, if $\Phi\subseteq\Phi'$, then
+$\Must(M,\Phi,\pi)\subseteq\Must(M,\Phi',\pi)$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Since $M\setminus\Phi'\subseteq M\setminus\Phi$, taking their images and then complements within the same $G$ reverses the inclusion.
+\end{proof}
+Expanding approved intent can remove an over-admission finding without changing the policy. The interpretation is an approved specification change, not a demonstrated policy hardening. A broader contract can be a legitimate project decision, but its approval is evidence that must be tracked separately from a policy edit.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Refining observations]
+\label{prop:refinement}
+Let $\rho:W\rightarrow Z$ be a finer observation and suppose $\pi=f\circ\rho$ for some $f:Z\rightarrow Y$. With fixed $M$ and $\Phi$, the states admitted by the greatest coarse safe set are contained in those admitted by the greatest fine safe set.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Take a state admitted by the coarse safe set. Every mintable state sharing its coarse observation is intended. Its fine observation class is a subset of that coarse class, so every state in the fine class is intended as well. Its fine observation belongs to the fine must-safe set.
+\end{proof}
+The result explains why adding a distinguishing claim can resolve a collision, but it does not assert that any particular issuer emits that claim or that the target policy supports testing it. The finite observation-basis routine enumerates subsets of explicitly supplied candidate coordinates, including the empty basis, and reports inclusion-minimal distinguishing bases. It separately reports the minimum cardinality. Inclusion-minimal does not imply globally minimum, and minimum among a supplied candidate set does not imply minimum across all possible provider extensions.
+
+For $k$ candidate coordinates, the routine explicitly considers at most $2^k$ subsets and rejects inputs with more than 16 candidates. Grouping $n$ supplied states by a chosen basis requires inspecting their selected coordinates. Superset pruning is sound because adding coordinates can split an observation class but cannot merge two previously separate classes. None of these finite computations is an automatic claim-key deployment procedure.
+
+\subsection{Why a safety certificate alone is not a full review receipt}
+\label{sec:omission}
+There is also a simple dependency argument. Fix a model with $G=H=\{m,r\}$ and policy $E=\{m\}$. A safety-only result is positive because $G\cap E\subseteq H$. With $Q_1=\{m\}$, the two-sided result is \Pass{}; with $Q_2=\{r\}$, it is \Fail{}. An evidence object that binds the same $G,H,E$ but omits $Q$ cannot distinguish these packets. A reusable \emph{two-sided} assertion must bind the positive requirements or reestablish them from an independently bound contract.
+
+A similar example applies to time. A fixed packet can satisfy the freshness inequality at $t_1$ and violate it at $t_2$. An assertion that binds the bytes but neither checks current freshness nor establishes an appropriate validity interval cannot be a current review judgment. These examples motivate the receipt design in Section~\ref{sec:replay}; they do not imply that a content digest is an authenticated attestation.
+
+The implementation deliberately reruns the current gate rather than implementing a proved incremental algorithm for these cases. Dependency information supports review explanations and rejection of stale reuse, not a claimed asymptotic improvement. An incremental extension would need to prove that every skipped obligation is unaffected by all changed semantic dependencies, including time and implementation changes. That proof is outside the present prototype.
