+\section{Analysis of the Supported Policy Fragment}
+\label{sec:analysis}
+\subsection{Preflight before interpretation}
+The strict wrapper accepts an explicit, auditable subset of policy syntax. It requires the explicit policy version \code{2012-10-17}; explicit Allow or Deny effects; one exact GitHub OIDC provider principal across the analyzed statements; the web-identity assumption action; and scalar subject/audience conditions formed with \code{StringEquals} or \code{StringLike}. A list of values is a disjunction within a condition key, while condition keys and operators in a statement are combined conjunctively. The AWS condition documentation is the external reference for these operator distinctions~\cite{iam-operators,aws-conditions}.
+
+\code{StringEquals} is literal matching. \code{StringLike} recognizes the star and question-mark glob constructs used by the implementation; brackets are literal characters, not a character-class extension inherited from a host-language glob library. Interpolation, unknown fields or operators, unsupported actions, ambiguous principals, and unsupported issuer profiles prevent a positive strict judgment. At least one Allow statement is required by the implemented profile. Optional policy \code{Id} and statement \code{Sid} fields must be strings. A missing version is not silently replaced with a default. An empty condition string denotes the empty literal; it is distinct from an empty list of alternatives, which is outside the accepted profile.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{The strict fragment is a precondition, not a claim of complete IAM support.}
+\label{tab:fragment}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.23\linewidth}XX@{}}
+\toprule
+Dimension & Supported here & Outside this strict profile \\
+\midrule
+Issuer & Declared legacy subject/audience constructor profile & Arbitrary custom or immutable profiles \\
+Principal & One exact compatible provider ARN across statements & Mixed provider principals or general principal logic \\
+Action/effect & Web-identity assumption; explicit Allow/Deny & Other actions and policy families \\
+Conditions & Scalar sub/aud; equals/like; explicit lists & Other keys/operators or source interpolation \\
+Environment premise & Supplied reviewed governance consistent with intent & Inferred or authenticated repository enforcement \\
+Source representation & Complete normalized policy in a packet & Automatic evaluation of arbitrary IaC programs \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+Requiring a common exact principal avoids discarding a principal distinction when the backend receives only the modeled subject/audience relation. If two statements refer to different provider ARNs, merging them into one observation policy can combine admissions that do not belong to the same authority. The revised preflight rejects this case rather than claiming support without a principal coordinate. The restriction is conservative and explicit; it does not assert that a richer analyzer could not handle the case.
+
+\subsection{Symbolic upper-bound analysis}
+The inherited analyzer represents the supported subject and audience languages with finite automata. Typed subject construction separates branches, tags, pull-request contexts, and environments. It intersects mintable observations with policy admissions and searches for witnesses outside the intended language, retaining the conjunction of condition coordinates and the effect of explicit denials. A discovered witness is a model-level counterexample: a tuple accepted by the modeled effective policy and excluded by the supplied upper bound.
+
+The core supports an older upper-bound interpretation that can conservatively erase certain unsupported restrictions from Allow statements. Removing an Allow restriction enlarges the accepted language, so proving the enlarged language safe can establish a one-sided safety property under the other modeled assumptions. This monotonic argument does not justify admitting unsupported syntax in the complete strict review. It also does not justify erasing restrictions from Deny statements: enlarging denial can hide an actual admission. The strict wrapper therefore rejects unsupported constructs before invoking the core, and the manuscript does not convert a permissive legacy-core result into a full two-sided \Pass{}.
+
+This separation resembles a standard static-analysis distinction between an abstraction and the concrete semantics that it is intended to approximate~\cite{abstract-interpretation}. Here the implementation boundary is particularly important because the positive obligation needs an actual modeled membership answer for each required tuple. An over-approximation adequate for proving absence of over-admission can be inadequate for proving that a required identity is accepted. We do not claim a mechanized abstraction relation between the entire Python implementation and the cloud provider.
+
+Automata operations can have unfavorable worst-case state growth. Product exploration and language difference are not constant-time operations, and there is no basis in the present experiments for asserting practical scaling across arbitrary policies. A monotonic analysis deadline is shared by the backend invocation and the subsequent positive checks; the remaining budget, rather than the original duration, is passed to the subprocess. Exceeding it yields insufficient evidence, not a declaration that the policy violates the contract. The finite literal-policy experiment records execution costs only for the tested inputs and includes process-launch and certificate-checking overhead.
+
+\subsection{Complete support for the modeled character domain}
+\label{sec:character-support}
+A finite search alphabet is sound only when it represents every character distinction made by the analyzed languages. The earlier implementation collected input literals and searched a fixed preferred pool for a fresh character. When every preferred character was already literal, its fallback retained that same pool. A character outside the pool could still match a wildcard, so the fallback omitted a nonempty semantic class. Analyzer/replayer agreement could not detect the common omission. The correction changes support construction in the retained core, rather than narrowing the scalar matcher to the deficient alphabet.
+
+We make the domain explicit: $\Gamma=\{\mathrm{chr}(k)\mid 0\leq k<\mathtt{0x110000}\}$, the Python string code-point domain~\cite{python-unicode}. The model does not normalize Unicode, match grapheme clusters, or interpret a code point as a UTF-8 byte. This is not a statement that a provider issues every modeled string. The strict packet transport separately requires UTF-8-encodable input; an isolated surrogate in a packet yields an invalid-input \Unknown{}. Direct-library tests may nevertheless exercise such Python strings to check the primitive domain without claiming they are valid provider tokens.
+
+Let $S$ contain every literal character in the issuer, policy and intent, together with every fixed constructor literal and the boundaries of its character predicates. In this implementation, the default owner/repository predicate excludes slash, colon, star and question mark; the suffix predicate excludes colon, star and question mark. These boundary characters are singleton classes even when a policy only uses star and question mark as operators. In \code{StringEquals} they are also ordinary literal characters. Define
+\begin{equation}
+A=S\cup\{r\}\quad\text{where }r\in\Gamma\setminus S,
+\qquad
+\pi(c)=\begin{cases}c,&c\in S,\\r,&c\notin S.\end{cases}
+\label{eq:complete-character-support}
+\end{equation}
+When $S=\Gamma$, set $A=S$ and $\pi$ to the identity. Extend $\pi$ to strings by applying it to each character. Preferred characters only influence the concrete choice of $r$; exhausting their pool does not remove the residual class. The revised selector continues over a disjoint enumeration of the complete code-point domain. It may omit a residual representative only when that domain is actually exhausted.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Support completeness for the implemented predicates]
+\label{prop:character-support}
+Suppose $S$ contains the literal singleton tests and every finite exclusion used by the constructor automata, and every other consuming transition is unrestricted. For each issuer, equality, or star/question-mark language $L$ built from these predicates, $x\in L$ if and only if $\pi(x)$ is accepted by its restriction to $A$. Consequently, support restriction preserves the emptiness and containment judgments used by the effective subject/audience analysis.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+For a singleton predicate $c=a$, the tested character $a$ belongs to $S$. A character outside $S$ cannot equal $a$, and neither can its representative. An excluded-character predicate has its complete exclusion set in $S$; all characters outside $S$, including $r$, therefore have identical membership. An unrestricted transition accepts both, and an epsilon transition consumes neither. Thus replacing an input character by its representative preserves every enabled transition. Induction on the length of a run establishes both directions of NFA membership; epsilon closure does not alter the argument.
+
+Union, intersection and complement-relative-to-$\Gamma^*$ preserve this equivalence, as does difference. Apply the same map separately to the subject and audience coordinates. Every concrete effective-admission violation therefore has a representative violation, while every representative violation is itself a concrete string pair in the declared domain. This proves the required emptiness and containment equivalences. The argument depends on the stated primitive predicates: adding a new character class or normalization rule requires refining the partition, not merely reusing this theorem.
+\end{proof}
+
+The low-level NFA API remains a finite-alphabet API. Its membership operation takes representative words; it does not silently reinterpret an arbitrary out-of-support character as a literal. Concrete membership is evaluated either by the scalar matcher or by explicitly constructing the support and applying $\pi$. The analyzer and effective replayer add the constructor boundaries before search and check coverage before interpreting a result. Failure to establish coverage is an error at the backend boundary and hence insufficient evidence at the strict gate, not a safety result.
+
+The atomic certificate verifier now checks the declared domain, every required singleton, and the nonempty residual class before checking witness or invariant transitions. The residual check uses the domain cardinality and the supplied singleton set; it does not call the fresh-character selector. Atomic certificates use version 3 and effective-case certificates use version 6. Earlier versions require regeneration. A full-review receipt additionally binds the changed implementation identity, so a historical receipt cannot retain a positive judgment merely because its producer and replayer previously shared the same incomplete support.
+
+\subsection{Independent scalar evaluation of positive tuples}
+For each required tuple, the scalar checker first asks whether it is mintable under the selected profile and intended by the contract. It then evaluates every supported statement against the tuple and takes the disjunction of matching Allows minus any matching Deny. The check records the three Boolean components separately, along with the tuple's reason. A record outside the issuer domain or intent is classified as invalid even if the policy happens to reject it.
+
+The scalar path and symbolic path have different implementations but are not independent systems in a strong assurance sense. They share Python execution, input construction, profile assumptions, and parts of the surrounding validation. Their agreement on the literal and finitely bounded glob configurations is useful implementation evidence, not a proof of full semantic equivalence. We restrict the strict differential experiments so that a direct finite set oracle can represent every tuple their policies may accept. This restriction is established syntactically, not inferred from successful executions.
+
+\subsection{Exact scalar matching with explicit exhaustion}
+\label{sec:scalar-matching}
+The positive checker implements star/question-mark matching with a prefix dynamic program. This avoids inheriting bracket classes or escape conventions from a host-language glob utility, and avoids translating repeated stars into a backtracking regular expression. Let $p=p_1\cdots p_m$ be a pattern and $x=x_1\cdots x_n$ a scalar claim. Write $B_{i,j}$ for whether the first $i$ pattern characters match the first $j$ claim characters. Initialize $B_{0,0}=\mathsf{true}$ and $B_{0,j}=\mathsf{false}$ for $j>0$, and use
+\begin{equation}
+B_{i,j}=\begin{cases}
+B_{i-1,j}\lor(j>0\land B_{i,j-1}), & p_i=*,\\
+j>0\land B_{i-1,j-1}\land(p_i=?\lor p_i=x_j), & p_i\ne *.
+\end{cases}
+\label{eq:glob-dp}
+\end{equation}
+The expression for the second row is false when $j=0$, without reading a negative index. A star can consume no character; a question mark consumes exactly one. Every other character is literal, including brackets, backslashes, and line separators. The implementation operates on Python string characters, not UTF-8 bytes; this is a statement about its local string semantics, not the provider's complete Unicode normalization behavior.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Scalar matcher exactness]
+If the computation completes, Equation~\ref{eq:glob-dp} returns true exactly when the whole claim belongs to the language of the whole star/question-mark pattern.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The empty-pattern row gives the stated base case. For a literal or question mark, a match must consume the last claim character and leave a match of the two shorter prefixes, which is precisely the second case. For a star, either it consumes no character, leaving $B_{i-1,j}$, or it consumes at least one; removing its final consumed character leaves $B_{i,j-1}$. Both decompositions can also be extended to form a match. Induction in increasing $i$, and then increasing $j$ within a star row, establishes both directions for every cell. The returned cell is $B_{m,n}$, so the result is whole-string rather than substring membership.
+\end{proof}
+
+Only two rows are retained, giving $O(n)$ auxiliary space and $O(m(n+1))$ work for a wildcard match. A pattern without wildcards takes a literal-equality fast path. Wildcard matches exceeding the declared 2,000,000-cell work cap, or checks reaching the shared deadline, raise a named exhaustion condition. The strict gate and normalized finite adapter translate this condition to \Unknown{}, not to a negative membership answer. The bound is per scalar match; it is not a claim that JSON parsing, allocation, or all host execution is preempted by a hard real-time sandbox.
+
+The regular-language study interface additionally treats explicit issuer subject patterns as \emph{refinements} of the typed default constructor language. They do not replace that language with arbitrary custom subjects. The positive checker applies the same intersection and respects an explicit issuer-audience refinement. Absent or empty refinement lists retain the inherited defaults. The separate finite adapter continues to accept an explicitly supplied relation; its different interface is not used to extend the strict profile implicitly.
+
+\begin{algorithm}[t]
+\caption{Strict review of a supplied packet (the implementation retains detailed diagnostics)}
+\label{alg:review}
+\begin{algorithmic}[1]
+\Require Packet $P$, check time $t$, finite positive analysis budget $b$
+\State Validate contract, approval digest, snapshot digests, scopes, and freshness
+\If{a required premise is invalid or unsupported}
+    \State \Return \Unknown{} with diagnostic reasons
+\EndIf
+\State Compile the supported intent and check the strict policy fragment
+\State Run the existing upper-bound analyzer within the remaining budget
+\State Replay its safety evidence using the existing checker
+\State Within the same deadline, check each required tuple's mintability, intent, and policy membership
+\State $V\gets$ all premises valid, analysis completed, and replay agreed
+\State $O\gets$ a supported over-admission witness exists
+\State $L\gets$ a valid required tuple is not admitted
+\State Apply Equation~\ref{eq:decision}; preserve subordinate diagnostics
+\State Attach input and implementation dependencies; do not reuse a previous verdict
+\State \Return the result and its scope flags
+\end{algorithmic}
+\end{algorithm}
+
+\subsection{Finite study adapters are not a bypass for unsupported production input}
+Some supplied public cases describe non-legacy subjects or generated policy fragments. The existing study support includes a normalized finite adapter accepting an explicit issuer relation, Allow and Deny relations, intent, and required tuples. Its semantics are exact over those supplied finite sets. It does not infer additional mintable identities from an observed tuple and does not establish the completeness of the supplied issuer relation.
