+\section{Evidence Binding and Replay}
+\label{sec:replay}
+\subsection{Two different evidence objects}
+The inherited backend certificate concerns the upper-bound analysis. Its checker reconstructs relevant model and language operations using shared implementation components. It is useful for detecting certain result inconsistencies, but it does not independently authenticate inputs or, by itself, establish the positive requirement. A safety certificate that remains valid after deleting every required admission still says nothing about whether the release contract has been preserved.
+
+The journal revision adds a full-review receipt around the existing strict gate. The receipt schema contains the complete packet, a selected semantic view of the result, a false source-authenticity assertion, and a digest of the receipt body. The selected view contains the verdict, exit code, packet digest, input dependency hashes, fragment identifier, backend certificate digest, required-token checks, findings, and the backend replay status. Timing measurements and presentation-only text are not used as semantic equality criteria.
+
+The receipt constructor rejects a mismatch between a recorded packet digest and the supplied packet. The command-line review path reads the packet once and reuses that object when constructing the receipt, avoiding a second file read that could bind different bytes from those reviewed. These checks protect consistency within the local workflow. They are not protection against an adversary controlling both the program and the final output.
+
+\input{figures/receipt}
+
+\subsection{Replay means recomputation, not cached acceptance}
+Receipt replay performs three steps. First it validates the exact envelope fields, schema, declared authenticity boundary, and digest. Second it runs the current strict gate on the enclosed packet using the replay caller's current check time and analysis budget. Third it compares the recomputed semantic view with the recorded one. A malformed receipt or disagreement returns \Unknown{} with \code{receipt\_replay\_ok=false}. The report can include the current gate result to explain whether the disagreement arose from expiration, changed implementation dependencies, or another changed judgment.
+
+If the views agree, replay returns the recomputed result together with \code{receipt\_replay\_ok=true}. This flag means that the recorded and recomputed reviews agree; it does not necessarily mean the review passed. A faithfully replayed \Fail{} remains a failure, and a faithfully replayed \Unknown{} remains insufficient evidence. This distinction prevents a successful evidence-integrity check from being mistaken for a successful authorization check.
+
+An unchanged packet may produce a new \Unknown{} after its freshness window expires. A different implementation digest can also cause receipt disagreement even if the top-level verdict happens to remain \Pass{}. The receipt deliberately binds more than a status bit. Conversely, the workflow body may change without changing the policy-language judgment. That change still alters the dependency vector and requires a new review record. Dependency binding and semantic interpretation are separate responsibilities.
+
+\subsection{The joint freshness interval}
+\label{sec:freshness-interval}
+The four snapshots can be captured at different times. Let their declared UTC observation times be $s_1,\ldots,s_4$, and let the reviewed maximum age be $\tau>0$. The temporal premise at caller time $t$ is $0\leq t-s_i\leq\tau$ for every snapshot. Thus the usable interval is the intersection of four closed intervals, not a fresh-looking timestamp selected from one artifact.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Freshness intersection]
+All supplied snapshots satisfy the age premise exactly when
+\begin{equation}
+\max_i s_i\ \leq\ t\ \leq\ \min_i(s_i+\tau).
+\label{eq:freshness-intersection}
+\end{equation}
+If the lower endpoint exceeds the upper endpoint, no caller time satisfies all four declared observations.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The lower inequalities $0\leq t-s_i$ are equivalent to $t\geq\max_i s_i$. The upper inequalities $t-s_i\leq\tau$ are equivalent to $t\leq\min_i(s_i+\tau)$. Their conjunction proves the claim, including both endpoints and the empty-intersection case.
+\end{proof}
+
+\input{figures/freshness}
+
+For fixed bytes and timestamps, the temporal validation outcome is unchanged within this interval. Equality of the \emph{full} replay result additionally requires the same implementation and successful deterministic analysis within its resource budget; freshness alone cannot guarantee it. Crossing the upper endpoint invalidates the current review even if no input hash changes. A future-dated snapshot similarly prevents a judgment before the lower endpoint.
+
+Regression checks use staggered observation times, both exact interval endpoints, the next second outside the interval, and equivalent timezone offsets. Explicit malformed clock arguments are rejected: the API interprets only an omitted argument as a request for the current clock. An empty string, Boolean, or zero must not silently substitute a different time. These tests validate arithmetic on declared capture times, not their authenticity or the synchronization of a remote capture service.
+
+\subsection{What the digest establishes}
+The digest is computed from deterministic JSON serialization. Object key order is normalized, but list order remains part of the representation. Reordering semantically equivalent statements can therefore change packet identity. This is acceptable for a review artifact: it is intended to bind reviewed bytes and associated metadata, not to provide a canonical normal form for every semantically equivalent policy.
+
+A digest establishes a reproducible relation among supplied objects. It does not identify who produced them, establish their capture time, or prove their origin. An altered object can be rehashed. The replay tests therefore include cases in which a receipt's digest is recomputed after changing the decision; the current gate must still reject the inconsistent semantic claim. The security boundary is recomputation under a trusted local implementation, not secrecy of the hashing procedure.
+
+Proof-carrying code distinguishes producer-supplied evidence from a checker that validates a specified safety claim~\cite{pcc}; translation validation similarly focuses on validating individual transformations rather than proving every compiler execution correct~\cite{translation-validation}. These are useful conceptual comparisons, but \FF{} does not claim their assurance level merely by using the word ``certificate.'' Its backend and checker share a trusted core, and its full receipt invokes the same gate again. A common parser or automata bug can affect production and replay consistently.
+
+\subsection{Dependency completeness and implementation identity}
+The implementation dependency is derived from Python sources under the inherited core and the review-wrapper package, with relative file names and bytes included. It is not a digest of the entire machine, interpreter distribution, dependency supply chain, or operating-system image. The reproduction record separately reports the interpreter and platform. Changes to tests or manuscript text do not claim to change the implemented policy semantics, while changes to the core, wrapper, or receipt code invalidate recorded implementation identity.
+
+The contract dependency covers the positive set through the contract body. Snapshot dependencies cover both content and the associated review metadata used by the gate. The clock is re-evaluated rather than treated as a cached hash dependency. A previous result can support a dependency diff but cannot remove any of these obligations. If validation fails before dependency construction, the implementation may report no changed fields; that does not mean no input changed. The stale-snapshot experiment explicitly records this early-exit behavior.
+
+The current mechanism is intentionally conservative about reuse. It does not offer an incremental certificate cache and does not claim that all semantically irrelevant edits can be recognized. This choice trades potential speed for a smaller reuse argument: a current result is available only after current validation and analysis. The measured experiment is therefore an execution of the gate, not a benchmark of a hypothetical dependency-aware incremental algorithm.
+
+\subsection{Relationship to authenticated supply-chain evidence}
+Supply-chain mechanisms address related but different evidence questions. In-toto specifies and checks the steps and actors in a software supply chain~\cite{intoto}; Sigstore provides an ecosystem for software signing~\cite{sigstore}; TUF specifies authenticated update metadata and freshness-related protections~\cite{tuf}; and SLSA defines supply-chain requirements and provenance concepts~\cite{slsa}. None of these mechanisms automatically supplies a project's release-intent predicate or proves that an IAM condition admits exactly the desired claim relation.
+
+A future authenticated capture pipeline could protect the origin of \FF{} snapshots and receipts. That integration would strengthen the source premise, not replace the semantic conformance check. Likewise, adding a signature to a receipt would not correct an incomplete issuer model or an invalid positive requirement. The current artifact does not implement these integrations, and its reports do not mark their properties as verified.
