+\section{Implementation and Consistency Repairs}
+\label{sec:implementation}
+The artifact preserves two existing layers. The inherited \code{artifact/fedfence} package contains the subject grammar, policy interpretation, automata operations, analyzer, and safety-certificate support. The \code{fse\_workflow} package contains the review contract, strict preflight, backend invocation, positive conformance checks, comparison logic, reporting, and study adapters. The historical package name is retained to avoid creating an artificial second implementation for the journal version. The new receipt module composes these components rather than replacing the analyzer.
+
+The main workflow requires Python 3.10 or later and uses the standard library. The documented reproduction path does not install external cloud tools or request credentials. LaTeX compilation is a separate local step using \code{acmart}, BibTeX, TikZ, and \code{latexmk}. Optional historical source-processing utilities are not prerequisites for the current results. The README and Makefile identify current entry points; older reports and submission-specific assumptions are retained only as explicitly superseded history.
+
+\subsection{Correcting disagreements rather than preserving old counts}
+The supplied manuscript reported 79 tests, while executing its current test discovery found 94 passing tests before this revision. The discrepancy was a reporting problem, not evidence of 15 newly created tests. The first integration revision added 18 tests. A subsequent boundary audit added 34 tests covering scalar representation, issuer refinements, budget exhaustion, malformed clocks, and replay, producing 146 tests at that checkpoint. The character-domain correction adds 48 regression methods, producing the current \TestCount{}-test suite. Unlike the earlier integration-only changes, it updates support construction and certificate validation in the retained core. Table~\ref{tab:repairs} summarizes the semantic and integration changes that motivated the new checks.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Concrete consistency repairs in the retained implementation. These are audited changes, not a claim that every implementation defect has been eliminated.}
+\label{tab:repairs}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.23\linewidth}XX@{}}
+\toprule
+Boundary & Observed risk in the supplied integration & Revision and check \\
+\midrule
+Decision precedence & Invalid positive declarations could coexist with a supported-looking failure & Shared invalid-first rule in gate and study paths \\
+Principal projection & Different provider principals could lose their distinction & Reject mixed exact provider ARNs in strict preflight \\
+Positive recognizer & Tuple shape and constructor assumptions needed consistent validation & Nonempty scalar coordinates and explicit grammar checks \\
+Finite adapter & Iterables could be consumed; coercion and glob semantics could diverge & Materialize inputs once; reject coercion; preserve literal brackets \\
+Study replay & A matching status could hide changed admitted tuples or findings & Compare the complete normalized result \\
+Character support & A saturated preferred pool omitted a nonempty character class & Whole-domain residual selection; singleton and constructor-boundary checks \\
+Atomic replay & A certificate-supplied alphabet could omit transitions & Domain/coverage validation before invariant checking \\
+Strict replay & A safety certificate omitted the full positive review obligation & Full packet/result receipt with current-gate recomputation \\
+Source study & Roles and a variable's empty/nonempty branches were merged & Separate contracts and configurations with explicit denominators \\
+Reproduction & Old paper paths and hard-coded FSE limits disagreed with the package & Sibling paper target and current result-driven audit \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+The implementation also rejects nonfinite, nonpositive, Boolean, unrepresentably large, or otherwise invalid timeout values at the API boundary. A test covers the exact inclusive freshness limit and the next second. Receipt tests cover ordinary round trips, alteration of a required identity, expired input at replay, a changed implementation dependency, an unsealed mutation, and a rehashed but semantically false decision. These checks concern local record consistency; no test sends a token or request to a cloud endpoint.
+
+One inherited repair-audit assertion compared a token dictionary with a collection of tuples, making the intended membership check ineffective. The revision compares the actual subject/audience tuple. This matters to the sports-store role split: a cross-role probe should exercise membership, not succeed because two Python representations are always unequal. The revised study and repair scripts retain separate raw results so that a summary is not the only place where the distinction is visible.
+
+\subsection{Representation and model-boundary repairs}
+Two small representations exposed different judgments in the earlier integration. A scalar empty condition string and its singleton-list form denote the same literal, but the positive evaluator rejected the latter. With a required release identity, this changed a supported exclusion into \Unknown{}. The repaired validator permits empty strings inside a nonempty alternatives list, while continuing to reject an empty list or nonstring elements. The paired regression preserves the semantic distinction between an empty literal and missing syntax.
+
+The regular-language study's issuer refinement exposed another disagreement. The core intersects an explicit subject pattern with its typed constructor language; the positive checker had treated it as a replacement and had omitted the optional audience refinement. A listed custom string could therefore look mintable to the scalar path even though the core excluded it. The revision applies both coordinate refinements to the same modeled constructor domain. Empty refinement lists retain the core's default behavior rather than creating an accidental empty issuer. This repair does not add support for custom profiles to the strict gate.
+
+Clock and metadata checks are kept separate from policy truth. Explicit false-like clock inputs no longer request the current clock, governance source references must be strings, and optional policy identifiers have explicit types. Deep malformed objects or resource-representation errors are converted to an insufficient-input judgment at the gate boundary. These checks make failures interpretable; they do not turn the validator into a general memory or execution sandbox.
+
+\subsection{Executable traceability}
+The current driver runs unit tests, the inherited core self-check, the finite projection audit, relational checks, two-sided validation, the independent character-domain audit, the matcher/issuer/bounded-glob and local-cost experiments, seeded-fault challenges, the repair audit, and the source-backed studies. Each step writes a log and a machine-readable result. Quantitative macros and table rows are generated from those results into the sibling paper directory. The manuscript is not updated by substituting a claimed count into prose independently of the experiment output.
+
+The release package includes source-level change records relative to the supplied project. It also identifies historical directories whose contents were not freshly regenerated. This distinction is necessary because retaining previous outputs is useful for provenance, but their presence must not imply they are results of the current reproduction command. The evidence manifest records what actually ran, with exit status and local execution time for each step.
+
+No claim of a full independent security audit follows from this process. The regression obligations and revised integration were prepared within the same development process, including the AI assistance disclosed at the end of this manuscript. The purpose of the audit trail is to make their assumptions inspectable and their reported executions repeatable. It supports a reviewer who wishes to challenge a particular premise or add a counterexample, rather than requiring trust in a high-level completion label.
