+\section{Evaluation}
+\label{sec:evaluation}
+\subsection{Research questions and evidence units}
+The evaluation asks four questions. \emph{RQ1:} Does the executable judgment agree with the stated set semantics on exhaustively enumerated small models and constructed strict inputs? \emph{RQ2:} Do the review and replay boundaries respond correctly to selected changes in premises and seeded implementation defects? \emph{RQ3:} What conclusions are supported by the supplied public changes, maintenance records, and source excerpts once entry points and denominators are preserved? \emph{RQ4:} What local recomputation costs are observed on a declared family of benign policies with increasing positive obligations?
+
+These questions concern semantic consistency and evidence interpretation, not deployment outcomes. The testing-oracle literature distinguishes observed program behavior from an independent basis for judging its correctness~\cite{oracle}. We therefore specify the oracle for each experiment. Direct set operations provide expected results for finite relations and explicitly finite policy bounds. An independently structured recursive matcher supplies expected wildcard membership for the bounded matcher experiment. Handwritten regression assertions exercise named boundary cases. A public commit or comment supplies contextual evidence, not an independently labeled semantic oracle.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Evaluation layers and their noninterchangeable units. None is a live-cloud validation layer.}
+\label{tab:layers}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.23\linewidth}p{.18\linewidth}XX@{}}
+\toprule
+Layer & Unit & Oracle or comparison & Principal limit \\
+\midrule
+Regression suite & \TestCount{} tests & Explicit assertions & Selected behavior, shared implementation \\
+Core self-check & \CoreComparisons{} comparisons & Bounded words and symbolic fixtures & Small alphabet/length plus named cases \\
+Projection audit & \ProjectionCount{} models & Direct state and image sets & Four states, two observations \\
+Two-sided enumeration & \FiniteCount{} decisions & Direct tuple-set formula & Three atoms and explicit invalid flag \\
+Strict literal differential & \StrictCount{} configurations & Exact finite effective tuple sets & Literal policies over three tuples \\
+Bounded glob differential & \GlobStrictCount{} configurations & Recursive matching and tuple sets & Finite equality upper bounds \\
+Character partition & \DomainPredicateChecks{} signatures & Full code-point sweep over three supports & Primitive predicates, not all policies \\
+Quotient membership & \DomainMatcherCount{} pairs & Concrete recursive/scalar vs projected NFA & Declared alphabets and lengths \\
+Matcher agreement & \MatcherCount{} pairs & Recursive, scalar, and NFA matching & Declared alphabets and lengths \\
+Issuer membership & \IssuerCount{} checks & Typed NFA versus scalar membership & Fixed constructors/refinements \\
+Local costs & \ScalingRuns{} timed runs & Clock measurements and expected judgments & One local environment; descriptive only \\
+Premise changes & Six transformations & Declared expected judgment and dependencies & Hand-constructed relations \\
+Seeded faults & Eight isolated changes & Preselected regression assertions & Not a population mutation score \\
+Public changes & \PublicConfigurations{} configurations & Frozen source plus explicit reconstruction & No owner confirmation or live run \\
+Source frontier & \SourceCount{} excerpts & Local digest and availability checks & No full-source extraction \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+The experiment driver records Python and platform information and the implementation digest. Its metadata records that no network access or cloud accounts are used; the current command sequence contains only local Python programs and document compilation. Public-source material is read from the supplied artifact, not newly collected through accounts. Execution costs are retained in raw CSV/JSON files, but the experiment is not designed as a statistically sampled performance comparison.
+
+Property-based testing motivates broad input variation rather than relying solely on a few successful examples~\cite{quickcheck}. Our finite audits enumerate their small domains instead of sampling randomly. Metamorphic testing motivates checking declared relations between related executions when a broad real-world oracle is unavailable~\cite{metamorphic}. We use six explicit dependency transformations and separately report their expectations. The enumeration and transformations provide different evidence and are not combined into one success percentage.
+
+\subsection{Source-backed study protocol}
+The public study begins with the frozen manifests and patch snapshots in the submitted project. For each of nine selected commits, the driver checks the patch digest and the manifest's source-assertion strings before evaluating the reconstructed configuration. Two corrected cases preserve distinct role and variable instantiations. Every result identifies whether the legacy regular-language study adapter or the finite explicit-issuer adapter was invoked.
+
+We treat the release contracts in these cases as analyst reconstructions from the supplied materials, not as authenticated approvals by the repository owners. Agreement with an expected label stored beside a reconstructed input is not an independent accuracy measurement. Case-study guidance emphasizes retaining context and making the evidential chain explicit~\cite{runeson}. Work on mining GitHub also cautions against treating repository artifacts as uncomplicated proxies for development activity~\cite{kalliamvakou}. These considerations motivate our separate source, model, and execution records.
+
+The same restriction applies to source-frontier annotations and maintenance reports. The former records the availability and original annotations of 24 excerpts; the latter records 12 supplied metadata entries. Neither is a representative sample with a defined population denominator. We do not estimate prevalence, generalize the fraction of passing reconstructions, or treat a successful-run identifier as a reproduced workflow execution.
