+\subsection{RQ1: Finite and strict-path semantic consistency}
+\label{sec:validation}
+The \TestCount{} regression tests complete with zero failures, errors, or skips. The inherited automata self-check completes \CoreComparisons{} bounded and symbolic comparisons. Its bounded part uses a small alphabet and bounded word lengths, supplemented by named policy and projection examples. These counts describe local assertions; they do not cover every string or every policy accepted by the implementation.
+
+The projection audit examines \ProjectionCount{} models over four states and two observation values. For each model it compares supplied-state safety with the must-safe observation containment property. It also retains a counterexample to replacing the must-safe set with the existential intended image. The resulting evidence supports the implemented enumeration of Proposition~\ref{prop:safety}; the written proof, rather than the bounded count, establishes the unrestricted set identity.
+
+The two-sided audit uses three explicit benign tuples. It enumerates all subsets for five relations: issuer $G$, intent $I$, Allow $A$, Deny $D$, and required set $Q$. This gives $8^5=32{,}768$ relation combinations. Each is evaluated with the explicit invalid-premise flag both false and true, giving \FiniteCount{} decisions. The expected admitted set is $G\cap(A\setminus D)$. The expected judgment is \Unknown{} for an explicit invalid premise or $Q\nsubseteq G\cap I$; otherwise it is \Fail{} for over-admission or a missing required tuple, and \Pass{} for neither.
+
+The implemented adapter agrees on all decisions: \FinitePass{} are \Pass{}, \FiniteFail{} are \Fail{}, and \FiniteUnknown{} are \Unknown{}. These frequencies arise from the enumerated powersets and the doubled validity flag. They are not estimates of how often real projects pass or require manual review. Every 257th case is also selected for complete finite-certificate replay, yielding \FiniteReplay{} replay checks. This is deterministic sampling within an exhaustive decision experiment, not exhaustive replay of every record.
+
+The strict differential experiment uses three subject/audience tuples, including an intended \code{main} tuple, a \code{main} tuple with a different benign audience, and a \code{dev} tuple. It constructs each of the seven nonempty Allow subsets and all eight Deny subsets as exact-literal statements. Because the statements contain no globs, they cannot admit an additional tuple outside this explicit finite universe. The oracle can therefore compute the complete effective policy with direct set subtraction even though the gate's issuer grammar is not finite.
+
+All \StrictCount{} cases agree: \StrictPass{} pass and \StrictFail{} fail, and all complete the backend evidence replay. This experiment exercises packet validation, strict preflight, backend invocation, symbolic analysis, scalar positives, shared outcome resolution, and safety-certificate replay. It does not test arbitrary wildcard policies, every supported source form, or a live IAM evaluator. Raw rows include the Allow mask, Deny mask, statement count, expected and observed judgments, replay status, and execution time.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Additional bounded checks of the relational properties in Section~\ref{sec:relational}. Counts are generated from the executed audit.}
+\label{tab:relational}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lrl@{}}
+\toprule
+Property & Checked instances & Observed result \\
+\midrule
+\RelationalRows
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+\subsection{RQ1 continued: Matching, issuer refinements, and bounded globs}
+\label{sec:glob-evaluation}
+The matcher experiment exhausts 1,555 patterns of length zero through four over six characters: two literals, star, question mark, and the two brackets. It also exhausts 156 claim strings of length zero through three over five characters: the two literals, the two brackets, and a line separator. For each of the \MatcherCount{} pairs, an independent recursive oracle enumerates the lengths consumed by each star. Its answer is compared with both the revised prefix matcher and the preserved core NFA. All three agree. The archive records the alphabets, length bounds, matching/nonmatching partition, deterministic result-stream digest, and selected inspection rows. The executable generator regenerates the entire comparison; this is not a corpus of provider-issued tokens.
+
+A second check targets the issuer bridge directly. Twelve benign subject strings include branches, a tag, an environment, a pull-request subject, malformed suffixes, wildcard characters in literal positions, and a custom-profile-shaped string. Four audiences are crossed with eight subject-refinement settings and six audience-refinement settings. All \IssuerCount{} scalar answers agree with the preserved typed subject and audience NFAs. This exercises empty refinements and audience restrictions separately from arbitrary finite issuer relations. Agreement here supports the local bridge repair, not completeness of the live issuer model.
+
+To extend the strict-path experiment beyond literal-only syntax without giving the oracle an unjustified domain, each generated Allow statement intersects its wildcard conditions with explicit finite equality bounds. The bounds contain three branch subjects and two audiences, giving six possible tuples. A separately specified release intent contains only the \code{main} and \code{release} subjects at the release audience.
+
+\begin{proposition}[A complete finite bound for these fixtures]
+Suppose every Allow statement includes equality bounds that place its subject and audience in finite sets $S$ and $U$, respectively. Other predicates are conjunctive restrictions, and Deny statements only remove tuples. Then the complete effective policy is a subset of $S\times U$, regardless of the wildcard languages in the other conditions.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Every tuple accepted by an Allow satisfies its equality conjuncts and therefore belongs to $S\times U$. Taking the union of those Allow sets preserves containment in the bound. Subtracting the union of Deny sets cannot add a tuple. Hence enumerating $S\times U$ suffices for complete membership of these policies.
+\end{proof}
+
+The experiment crosses eight subject patterns, four audience patterns, three Deny modes, and two positive-obligation settings, producing \GlobStrictCount{} complete packets. Alternating subject/audience pattern combinations also include a separately bounded exact release Allow, so the experiment exercises Allow union as well as Deny subtraction. The patterns include an empty literal, adjacent stars, a question mark, and literal brackets. All \GlobStrictCount{} strict-gate results agree with direct tuple-set evaluation: \GlobStrictPass{} are \Pass{} and \GlobStrictFail{} are \Fail{}. Every backend certificate replays, and every positive check agrees with the oracle's required-set membership. Full packets and a CSV of oracle and gate judgments are retained.
+
+These experiments have distinct units and should not be summed into a headline benchmark size. The matcher oracle differs structurally from the prefix algorithm and the NFA, but it still encodes the same declared local language. The issuer comparison deliberately uses the inherited model as its reference. The strict experiment supplies complete finite policy bounds, not an independent implementation of all IAM semantics. Its result extends tested syntax coverage without extending the claimed provider or source-language coverage.
+
+\subsection{RQ2: Changed premises and evidence replay}
+The dependency experiment applies six transformations to a fixed, fresh benign packet. An unchanged packet remains \Pass{} with no changed dependencies. A workflow revision changes the workflow dependency while preserving \Pass{}; an issuer source-description change similarly changes the issuer dependency without changing the language judgment. Subject widening produces \Fail{} and identifies the policy dependency. A stale governance observation produces \Unknown{}. An explicitly reapproved intent expansion preserves \Pass{} while changing the contract dependency.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Observed results of the six dependency transformations. The stale case exits validation before a dependency diff is constructed.}
+\label{tab:dependencies}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}Xll@{}}
+\toprule
+Transformation & Outcome & Changed dependency reported \\
+\midrule
+Unchanged fresh packet & \Pass{} & None \\
+Workflow revision bytes & \Pass{} & Workflow \\
+Issuer source metadata & \Pass{} & Issuer \\
+Widened subject condition & \Fail{} & Policy \\
+Expired governance snapshot & \Unknown{} & Not computed \\
+Approved intent expansion & \Pass{} & Contract \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+The stale case is especially important for interpreting output. An empty \code{changed\_fields} list after early validation failure is not evidence that no field changed. The raw result explains the failed freshness premise. Likewise, the unchanged outcome after a workflow edit is not evidence that the workflow is semantically equivalent: workflow semantics are not analyzed. These two observations prevent a dependency report from being promoted into a stronger program-analysis claim.
+
+Receipt regressions check that an expired snapshot, changed implementation identity, altered positive obligation, or rehashed false decision cannot silently reuse an earlier positive result. Finite-certificate regressions alter the admitted tuples while preserving the recorded status and require replay to detect the difference. A round-trip check additionally confirms that a well-formed receipt can be replayed without manufacturing a disagreement from irrelevant timing data.
+
+Eight hand-selected seeded semantic faults provide a further sensitivity check. Each fault is applied in an isolated temporary copy, with a named existing regression test run first on the unmodified code and then on the changed code. The faults remove invalid-first priority, omit positive loss, skip expiration, merge provider principals, coerce tuple coordinates, compare finite replay by status only, skip the receipt digest, or compare strict receipts by verdict only. All eight unmodified controls pass, and all eight faulty versions are detected by assertion failures rather than import or syntax errors.
+
+This result is not a representative mutation-adequacy score. The faults and detecting tests were chosen together to challenge named boundaries, and no random mutant population or equivalent-mutant analysis was performed. Their contribution is inspectability: a reviewer can see which semantic mistake each regression is sensitive to, rather than inferring coverage from the total unit-test count.
+
+\subsection{RQ4: Local recomputation costs}
+\label{sec:local-costs}
+The local-cost experiment varies the number of intended branch identities and required positives together over $1,2,4,8,16,32$. The policy has one equality-bounded Allow with those subjects. A paired loss variant adds one exact Deny excluding the first required identity. For each size, we exercise a passing review, the positive-loss review, and a full passing-receipt replay. These are executions of the existing gate, not an incremental cache or a competing tool.
+
+One untimed warm-up per size and task precedes three recorded repetitions. Task order rotates across repetitions to avoid always assigning a task the same position. This yields \ScalingRuns{} recorded runs and 18 warm-ups. Each run checks its expected judgment and backend replay status; full-receipt runs also require receipt agreement. Table~\ref{tab:local-costs} reports medians, and Figure~\ref{fig:local-costs} shows the observed minimum-to-maximum range. The archive records all measurements, interpreter/platform details, and the fixed input-generation protocol. Process startup, symbolic analysis, evidence checking, and scalar positives are included.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Observed median local cost in seconds, with three recorded repetitions per cell. Sizes vary together in the Allow list and positive set. These are descriptive measurements, not a comparison of tools.}
+\label{tab:local-costs}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrr@{}}
+\toprule
+Required identities & Passing review & Positive-loss review & Full receipt replay \\
+\midrule
+\ScalingRows
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+\input{figures/local-costs}
+
+A full receipt recomputes the gate, so the measured replay path does not promise a cached-speed advantage. The experiment varies only a small literal policy family; it does not stress all automata constructions, arbitrary wildcard intersections, source extraction, or a deployment service. Three repetitions in one local environment cannot establish tail latency, a confidence interval, a production service-level objective, or a population scaling law. Variation on a shared host and the policy family itself can dominate small differences between the tasks. The practical conclusion is limited to the recorded executions: all selected sizes complete with the specified judgments, and the associated costs are available for inspection.
+
+
+\subsection{Character-domain completeness and independent checks}
+\label{sec:character-validation}
+Table~\ref{tab:character-domain} records three deliberately saturated supports. For each, the audit visits all 1,114,112 Python code points, checking the literal singleton signature and the two constructor exclusion predicates before and after projection. This gives \DomainPredicateChecks{} primitive-signature comparisons. It is a complete enumeration of those character predicates for three supports, not of strings, automata runs, or policies. The separately written argument in Proposition~\ref{prop:character-support} is what extends the transition-preservation obligation to arbitrary finite words under the stated language construction.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Saturated support checks. Constructor boundaries are included in the singleton count. The final column is a code point chosen outside the singleton set, not an assertion of provider validity.}
+\label{tab:character-domain}
+\input{generated/character_domain_rows}
+\end{table}
+
+A second audit enumerates 259 patterns of length at most three and 111 concrete words of length at most two, under both equality and glob interpretation. The words include characters outside the preferred pool, supplementary code points, a line separator, an isolated surrogate, and literal metacharacters. Crucially, the concrete word is \emph{not} added to the search support. The comparison checks the concrete recursive oracle and scalar matcher against NFA membership of the explicitly projected word. All \DomainMatcherCount{} comparisons agree. The earlier \MatcherCount{}-comparison finite-alphabet test remains useful, but alone could not expose omission of an unlisted character class.
+
+A further \DomainConstructorCount{} checks insert twelve characters at five constructor positions and compare concrete scalar recognition with the representative-word NFA. Four regular-language review inputs pair the saturated subject/audience checks with closed-language positive controls. An independently selected concrete tuple confirms each negative obligation using scalar issuer, policy and intent predicates. Three complete strict packets exercise the corrected \Fail{}, the closed-control \Pass{}, and a stale-input \Unknown{}; their full receipts reproduce the same status. These are benign synthetic configurations, not provider-issued tokens or cloud deployments.
+
+Finally, two hand-selected local fault challenges check the rejection mechanism itself. Suppressing the residual selector is detected by the independent coverage check; bypassing certificate coverage defeats a deliberately incomplete-support rejection obligation whose unmodified control passes. These challenges address the reported common-mode defect. They are not an estimate of defect prevalence, mutation adequacy, or the independence of the whole trusted computing base.
