+\toprule
+Case group & Role/model unit & Before & After & Interpretation \\
+\midrule
+Six scope restrictions & One selected role each & \Fail{} & \Pass{} & Supported in the regular-language study model \\
+Environment restriction & One selected role & \Fail{} & \Unknown{} & Missing governance premise \\
+Sports-store ECR & ECR role & \Fail{} & \Pass{} & Explicit seven-tuple issuer relation \\
+Sports-store frontend & Frontend role & \Pass{} & \Pass{} & No violation in the same finite relation \\
+Microticket, empty branch & Deploy role & \Fail{} & \Fail{} & Required identity still absent \\
+Microticket, bound branch & Same deploy role & Same before & \Pass{} & Conditional binding, not observed assignment \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+\subsubsection{Sports-store: one commit is not one role contract}
+The sports-store snapshot contains distinct ECR and frontend roles~\cite{change-sports}. The corrected reconstruction therefore maintains two role contracts. Its explicit issuer relation contains seven supplied tuples: six service tuples and one frontend tuple. Under that finite relation, the ECR role changes from \Fail{} to \Pass{}, while the frontend role is \Pass{} both before and after.
+
+The frontend result does not show that its broader before-pattern is universally safe under the full provider grammar. It shows that no out-of-intent admission is exposed by the particular seven-tuple issuer relation. Adding another mintable frontend context could change the outcome. This is why the source driver records the finite entry point and its domain alongside the result instead of reporting a second general hardening success.
+
+The revised repair audit also checks role-specific tuple membership using tuple representations on both sides of the comparison. Such a probe would be meaningless if a token dictionary were compared against a tuple collection and declared absent because of the representation mismatch. The change strengthens the local regression check without adding evidence of an actual role assumption or deployment.
+
+\subsubsection{Microticket: a conditional source expression is not a resolved input}
+The microticket snapshot concerns acceptance of a reported immutable subject form~\cite{change-microticket}. The strict legacy gate does not claim support for that issuer profile. The study uses two explicitly supplied issuer tuples and separates the after-source's empty-default variable branch from a branch in which the variable is bound to the reported subject.
+
+The before reconstruction fails because the required immutable tuple is not admitted. The after-default-empty reconstruction also fails. The after-observed-binding reconstruction passes only conditional on a nonempty assignment matching the reported tuple. The supplied evidence does not establish that this variable was actually assigned in the relevant execution. A public success-run identifier stored in the metadata does not resolve the missing assignment, and the local study does not replay that run.
+
+These three configurations belong to one role contract and one commit. Counting them as three independent public projects would overstate diversity; collapsing them into a single ``after passes'' result would overstate certainty. The additional configuration is therefore reported explicitly and excluded from the ordinary before/after phase count.
+
+\subsubsection{What replay and source hashes contribute}
+The study checks all nine supplied patch digests and source-assertion strings. All 21 configurations complete the applicable safety or full finite-study replay check. Fourteen are analyzed through the regular-language study adapter and seven through the explicit finite adapter. The replay guarantees differ: a legacy safety-certificate check is not automatically a replay of the full strict packet contract.
+
+These checks establish local agreement among the frozen input, the declared reconstruction, and the executed adapter. They do not authenticate the source origin, confirm owner intent, or provide a second independent oracle. The study is best interpreted as a collection of inspectable examples of review obligations and evidence boundaries. Its most useful negative result is that several apparently convenient aggregate statements---all changes repaired, all configurations strict-gate supported, or all after-inputs resolved---are not supported by the supplied material.
