+\subsection{Source extraction and maintenance evidence}
+\label{sec:frontier}
+\subsubsection{Twenty-four excerpts are not twenty-four extractor executions}
+The source-frontier manifest contains \SourceCount{} frozen excerpts. Their local hashes all match. The current package contains \SourceFull{} complete source files for these records, and the revision performs \SourceExtractions{} full-source extractions. We therefore report inherited annotations separately from executable extraction outcomes. Absence of full context does not prove that a program is impossible to normalize, and the presence of a literal-looking excerpt does not prove that an extractor handles the complete source correctly.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Inherited source-frontier annotations, retained as annotations only. No row is an executed extraction success or failure.}
+\label{tab:frontier}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
+\toprule
+Original primary annotation & Records \\
+\midrule
+\FrontierRows
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+Seven records are annotated as literal source policy or constant-foldable HCL; the other seventeen have other annotations. The ratio $7/24$ is not extractor accuracy, coverage, or a population estimate. Full-file context, variable bindings, module instantiation, and host-language behavior can change what a fragment denotes. Terraform's JSON representation distinguishes configuration and evaluated values and includes information relevant to unknown or planned values~\cite{terraform-json}; a string copied from source is not necessarily the final evaluated trust policy.
+
+The frozen source records include examples attributed to the Babbel Terraform role module, the init4tech Pulumi/Go constructor, the Ad Hoc role module, and Tyk's infrastructure source~\cite{frontier-babbel,frontier-init4tech,frontier-adhocteam,frontier-tyk}. They illustrate different forms of context that a normalizer might need. Their bibliography entries preserve supplied revision identifiers; the revision verifies the artifact's local excerpts rather than claiming that these complete remote files were fetched and analyzed anew.
+
+\input{figures/evidence}
+
+An earlier source-frontier audit could classify unavailable full context as a requirement for instantiation. That interpretation confuses an evidence-access limitation with a semantic property of the program. The current output uses \code{not-executed} for extraction and a separate full-source-availability field. This makes the negative conclusion precise: the package does not presently support an empirical full-source extraction claim.
+
+\subsubsection{Maintenance reports and issuer-profile drift}
+The maintenance manifest contains \MaintenanceCount{} supplied records with matching local record digests. \ReportedBreakages{} carry observations annotated as breakage rather than hardening, and \RecordedSuccesses{} include a public success-run identifier. The local revision executes no public workflow and independently adjudicates no root cause. The count of reported breakages is consequently a property of the stored metadata, not a verified failure rate.
+
+Records attributed to CaringalML, OASI-CL, order-pools, dst-server-manager, and linyanqing describe subject-format or trust-policy compatibility observations~\cite{change-caringal,change-oasi,change-orderpools,change-dst,change-linyanqing}. Additional records attributed to danish09, jarvis, linkforge, and vicdf contain related reports or diagnostic references~\cite{change-danish,change-jarvis,change-linkforge,change-vicdf}. We use these as supplied maintenance narratives. We do not claim to have decoded the referenced live tokens, inspected CloudTrail in those accounts, or verified that an issuer rollout affected all repositories.
+
+Their engineering implication is narrower and useful: an issuer profile is a versioned input whose mismatch can invalidate a trust-policy review, and an observed positive identity can motivate a regression requirement. This does not establish the completeness of an issuer model from one observed token. It also does not show that a selected source edit caused a successful run. A future field study would need authenticated configuration snapshots, resolved variables, independently defined contracts, and a documented observation process before making those stronger claims.
