+\section{Using the Result in a Change-Review Workflow}
+\label{sec:integration}
+\subsection{Review obligations, not automatic policy repair}
+A useful workflow starts with the contract rather than the diff. The reviewer identifies permitted release contexts and chooses the positive identities whose policy-level admission must survive a change. The packet then associates the policy with the relevant issuer, workflow, and governance snapshots. The checker reports whether those inputs support a judgment and, if so, whether both conformance obligations hold.
+
+For a supported upper-bound failure, a witness identifies a modeled admitted tuple outside intent. The reviewer can narrow the policy or explicitly change the contract through the separate approval process. For a missing positive identity, the reviewer can restore its admission or remove the obligation only through an intentional contract revision. These actions are different from accepting a diagnostic simply because the policy was narrowed. \FF{} does not automatically decide which release behavior the project should authorize.
+
+For an invalid required identity, a policy edit may be the wrong action. A misspelled subject, an unsupported issuer format, or a requirement outside the intended release scope indicates an inconsistent packet. The appropriate response is to reconcile the evidence and specification. Similarly, an environment collision requires a justified governance or observation change; generating a different spelling of the same environment pattern cannot separate indistinguishable states.
+
+The finite basis enumeration can explain which supplied coordinates distinguish intended from unintended states. Its output is advisory model evidence. It neither modifies an issuer configuration nor installs a cloud policy, and it does not prove that a candidate coordinate is supported by the intended provider. The artifact's automated work remains restricted to static local material and benign constructed inputs.
+
+\subsection{A reproducible local review}
+The following commands operate on the provided benign fixture, not a live workflow. The historical check time is intentional: the snapshots are dated fixtures and would otherwise expire. The first command generates a full-review receipt; the second recomputes it using the same fixture time.
+\begin{lstlisting}[float=tbp,caption={Local strict review and full-receipt replay from the artifact directory.}]
+python3 -m fse_workflow review \
+  examples/release_gate/availability_before.json \
+  --now 2026-09-23T01:00:00Z \
+  --receipt-json tosem/results/example_receipt.json \
+  --result-json tosem/results/example_review.json
+
+python3 -m fse_workflow replay \
+  tosem/results/example_receipt.json \
+  --now 2026-09-23T01:00:00Z
+\end{lstlisting}
+The paired \code{availability\_after.json} fixture removes a required \code{main} identity while retaining the declared upper bound. Comparing the pair returns a supported required-identity regression. This is not a demonstration that a job fails after deployment; it is a reproducible demonstration that the modeled trust policy excludes the required tuple.
+
+An operational use would supply genuine approved inputs and use current time. Whether those inputs are captured and authenticated adequately is a responsibility outside the local gate. A CI wrapper should preserve the difference between exits 1 and 2, even when both block automatic continuation. Swallowing an \Unknown{} exit and displaying a success badge would defeat the evidence boundary without changing the analyzer.
+
+\subsection{Managing model drift}
+A stored issuer profile should be treated as a dependency that can become invalid, not as an eternal fact about a service. A newly observed subject form is evidence that a particular model may need revision. It is not evidence that all other forms have disappeared, that a finite observed set is complete, or that the policy language supports every useful identity attribute. The maintenance corpus illustrates the need to retain this distinction, without establishing a population-wide rollout history.
+
+A change in governance can likewise affect mintability independently of a policy edit. Under a restriction, an old safety obligation may become easier while a positive tuple becomes unmintable. Under an expansion, a formerly safe observation can gain an unintended preimage. Both changes require reestablishing the model-level obligations. The current implementation's full recomputation avoids pretending that a previous policy-only certificate settles either case.
+
+The reviewer can also change the required set as the release process evolves. A finite positive set is a deliberate selection of regression obligations, not a complete characterization of business continuity. Losing an unlisted release identity need not produce \Fail{} if it remains merely permitted rather than required. This limitation should be understood when selecting $Q$; the tool cannot infer an omitted requirement from the absence of a failing check.
+
+\subsection{Practical relevance without an unmeasured productivity claim}
+The design turns implicit assumptions into reviewable objects: the upper bound, positive identities, governance premises, issuer profile, and validity interval. It also distinguishes a request for policy repair from a request for better evidence. These are concrete workflow capabilities demonstrated by the artifact's inputs and outputs. They do not establish that developers complete reviews faster, make fewer mistakes, or prefer this process to existing tools.
+
+A human study would need participants, task design, an independently judged outcome, and controls for familiarity and tool configuration. A deployment study would need authenticated snapshots and permission to observe actual systems. Neither was performed. The present contribution is a method and an auditable local implementation that such studies could evaluate, rather than a simulated user-efficiency result written as an empirical finding.
