+\section{Related Work and Scope of the Research Increment}
+\label{sec:related}
+\subsection{Access-control verification and change analysis}
+Margrave supports verification and change-impact analysis of access-control policies~\cite{margrave}. It is directly relevant to the observation that a policy edit should be understood semantically rather than only textually. \FF{} does not claim to introduce policy containment or semantic differencing. Its emphasis is the review packet that connects a trust policy to a separate release intent, positive regression identities, supplied mintability and governance premises, and a current evidence boundary.
+
+Zelkova applies SMT-based automated reasoning to AWS access policies~\cite{zelkova}. That work establishes a substantial prior foundation for semantic reasoning about cloud authorization. Our restricted regular-language backend should not be described as a more general or more powerful replacement. \FF{} studies how a supported policy result participates in a cross-artifact change review, including the cases where the surrounding assumptions do not support a judgment.
+
+AWS also documents custom policy checks for whether an update grants new access, whether specified access is ungranted, and whether a resource policy grants public access~\cite{aws-custom-checks}. These are closer to semantic change review than syntax validation alone. A no-new-access relation is not by itself a positive-preservation condition: removing a required identity can satisfy a nonexpansion check. \FF{} therefore states its finite lower obligation explicitly. This distinguishes the obligations being evaluated; it is not evidence that an existing policy-analysis service could not express a corresponding check or that \FF{} outperforms it.
+
+Cedar provides an authorization language with a formal semantics and analysis facilities~\cite{cedar}. Its verification-guided development work combines formal models, proofs, and implementation testing~\cite{cedar-vgd}. The comparison is particularly important for assurance claims. Our bounded audits and shared-core replay are weaker evidence than a mechanized correspondence argument. The journal revision therefore reports where a direct set oracle is used, where implementations are shared, and where a model-to-provider bridge remains unproved.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Relation to neighboring approaches. This is a scope comparison based on the cited work and documentation, not an empirical ranking or a claim that the listed systems lack extensibility.}
+\label{tab:related}
+\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{.23\linewidth}XX@{}}
+\toprule
+Approach & Relevant focus & Relationship to this study \\
+\midrule
+Margrave~\cite{margrave} & Policy verification and change impact & Prior semantic change reasoning; \FF{} adds its specific cross-artifact review contract \\
+Zelkova~\cite{zelkova} & Semantic AWS policy reasoning & Stronger/general neighboring policy-analysis setting; no comparative execution here \\
+Cedar~\cite{cedar,cedar-vgd} & Authorization semantics and verification-guided implementation & Reference for distinguishing proofs, models, and implementation evidence \\
+IaC analysis~\cite{seven-sins,iac-replication,gasel} & Configuration weaknesses and richer source analysis & A source-level diagnostic is different from a two-sided intent judgment \\
+ARGUS~\cite{argus} & Staged taint analysis for workflows and actions & Workflow injection analysis is distinct from claim-relation conformance \\
+Supply-chain evidence~\cite{intoto,sigstore,slsa} & Provenance, signing, and process evidence & Potential source-premise support, not a substitute for the release contract \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table}
+
+\subsection{Infrastructure-as-code quality and configuration verification}
+The Seven Sins study identifies security smells in infrastructure-as-code scripts~\cite{seven-sins}. Its replication in Ansible and Chef examines the transfer of smell categories and detection to other IaC settings~\cite{iac-replication}. The Gang of Eight taxonomy studies a broader range of IaC defects~\cite{gang-eight}. These works provide context for configuration quality, but a smell is not automatically a demonstrated violation of a particular project's release contract. Conversely, a required-identity regression can matter even when a policy contains no generic smell.
+
+Opdebeeck et al. study the role of control and data flow in security-smell detection for IaC~\cite{gasel}. Their concern reinforces why complete source context and evaluated bindings cannot be replaced by a short excerpt. Our source-frontier audit does not reproduce that kind of full-source analysis. It reports the evidence actually available and avoids presenting stored classifications as an extractor benchmark.
+
+Rehearsal gives Puppet configurations a formal account and analyzes properties such as determinacy and idempotency~\cite{rehearsal}. It exemplifies semantic verification of configuration behavior rather than inspection of isolated strings. \FF{} has a different semantic object: a supplied issuer/policy relation and release contract, not the operational behavior of a general configuration language. Extending \FF{} to arbitrary Terraform or Pulumi execution would require a separate, justified source-semantics layer and is not accomplished by adding a few source examples.
+
+\subsection{Workflow security and policy-as-code tools}
+ARGUS performs staged static taint analysis of GitHub workflows and actions~\cite{argus}. Injection-oriented workflow analysis can identify a different class of risk from a trust-policy upper-bound check. A project might need both: an intended identity could execute an unsafe workflow, while a safe workflow program could be associated with an overly broad role trust policy. Binding a workflow snapshot in a \FF{} packet does not implement ARGUS-style reachability or taint analysis.
+
+IAM Access Analyzer policy-validation documentation describes checks against policy-writing rules and security considerations~\cite{aws-validation}. Checkov documents static analysis of infrastructure configurations~\cite{checkov}. OPA provides a policy language in which users can encode application-specific rules~\cite{opa}. These are relevant neighboring tools, but we executed none of them as a comparative baseline in this revision. In particular, behavior of a custom OPA rule would need to be attributed to both that rule and the engine; it would not establish a limitation of OPA's expressiveness.
+
+A fair comparison would normalize input capabilities, specify the same upper and lower obligations, identify unsupported cases, and distinguish a tool's built-in rules from experimenter-written adapters. It would also need an independent answer key. The present public reconstructions do not supply such an oracle, so they cannot support an accuracy or superiority table among products.
+
+\subsection{Specification, enforceability, and evidence}
+Classical program logic makes the role of explicit assertions central to reasoning about program behavior~\cite{hoare}. \FF{} adopts the corresponding discipline of separating intended behavior from the implementation being checked; it does not introduce a new program logic. Schneider's analysis of enforceable security policies distinguishes what can be enforced by an execution-monitoring mechanism~\cite{schneider}. Our finite positive obligation is not a liveness theorem: proving that a required tuple is admitted by a modeled policy does not prove that a workflow eventually runs successfully.
+
+Proof-carrying code and translation validation motivate explicit evidence and checking boundaries~\cite{pcc,translation-validation}, while secure-development guidance such as SSDF places verification activities within a broader development process~\cite{ssdf}. \FF{} occupies a narrow position in that process. It binds and checks a specified trust-change judgment, with explicit \Unknown{} outcomes for inadequate premises. It does not certify the entire supply chain, implementation, or release environment.
+
+The resulting research increment is methodological and implementational: a two-sided, snapshot-relative review contract; an observation account that exposes unexpressible distinctions; and a receipt/evaluation discipline that preserves the boundaries among source, model, checker, and runtime. The paper does not claim a new universal policy solver, a novel mathematical foundation for set containment, or evidence that these review mechanisms are unique across all possible authorization systems.
