+\section{Threats to Validity, Limitations, and Research Ethics}
+\label{sec:validity}
+\subsection{Construct and internal validity}
+The principal construct is conformance to a supplied release contract. It is not overall application security, cloud authorization success, or release availability. A \Pass{} is conditional on the correctness and completeness of the chosen issuer and governance model and on the contract reflecting the intended behavior. The positive set is finite and can omit important release paths. The upper bound can be too permissive if the contract itself is poorly chosen.
+
+The written propositions concern sets and projections. The bounded audits confirm selected executable instances but do not mechanically prove that the implementation refines the mathematical definitions. The strict experiments have deliberately complete finite oracles for literal policies and equality-bounded glob policies; they do not provide one for arbitrary wildcard policies. The bounded matcher comparison covers specified alphabets and lengths, while its general recurrence is justified separately by a written proof. The scalar and symbolic implementations share assumptions, and backend generation and replay share a trusted core. Correlated defects can survive agreement checks. The preferred-pool omission was one such defect: the revision now states the complete character domain, proves the predicate partition obligation, and checks it independently of fresh-character selection. This reduces that specific risk but does not establish universal implementation correctness or provider equivalence.
+
+The regression suite is selected by the developers, and the eight seeded faults are selected together with detecting tests. Those tests show sensitivity to particular mistakes, not representative defect-detection rates. Counts of enumerated combinations should not be interpreted as independent statistical observations. The high fraction of \Unknown{} outcomes in the powerset enumeration is driven by the experiment's validity settings and inconsistent required sets, not by expected operational usability.
+
+The full review receipt checks current recomputation under a local implementation. It is neither a cryptographic source attestation nor an independently implemented proof checker. The implementation digest covers selected source packages rather than the entire execution environment. Current-time checking prevents reuse of an expired assertion under the stated timestamps, but cannot prove those timestamps correspond to authentic source capture.
+
+\subsection{External validity and source provenance}
+The strict gate implements one versioned legacy subject/audience profile and a limited trust-policy fragment. It excludes unsupported policy syntax rather than guessing at semantics. It does not model full AWS authorization, custom issuer projections, workflow execution, or enforcement of repository governance. Results from a finite explicit-issuer adapter cannot be generalized to the provider's complete identity domain.
+
+The public changes are a small, purposively assembled, frozen corpus supplied with the project. The revision verifies local hashes and source-assertion strings but does not independently authenticate all remote origins or obtain owner-confirmed contracts. A stored commit title, reported CloudTrail observation, or success-run identifier is not an independently reproduced cloud event. The source-frontier records lack complete source context and support no extraction-accuracy conclusion. We therefore avoid population rates, confidence intervals for arbitrary denominators, and general claims about the prevalence of issuer drift.
+
+No external tool was executed for a comparative evaluation. Differences discussed in related work concern documented problem scope, not measured speed, recall, precision, or usability. The raw execution timings and the small size-series experiment are local costs for constructed inputs, with no representative workload sampling or controlled cross-tool environment. The three repetitions per cell support observed medians and ranges, not statistical confidence or tail-latency estimates. They support reproduction diagnostics, not a practical performance guarantee.
+
+\subsection{Conclusion validity and the remaining assurance boundaries}
+The evidence supports repeatability of the current local checks and consistency on their declared domains. It also supports specific source-model interpretations with explicit conditions, including a \Fail{}-to-\Unknown{} environment case and a variable branch whose assignment is unestablished. It does not imply that the full approach is ready to act as the sole authorization boundary in a production release system.
+
+There are three substantive extensions that would require new evidence. First, an independent or mechanized checker would reduce the shared-core assurance boundary. Second, authenticated snapshot capture and a justified workflow/governance model would strengthen the connection to actual deployment states. Third, a field or controlled user study with independently specified contracts would test practical effect and usability. These are not filled by extrapolating existing finite counts or relabeling public narratives as experiments.
+
+The absence of these extensions does not invalidate the bounded results. It determines which conclusions can be drawn from them. A reviewer can inspect the existing mechanism as a local trust-change review method while assessing whether its restricted scope and methodological contribution justify publication. The artifact makes that assessment possible without asking the reviewer to accept an unsupported claim of end-to-end deployment security.
+
+\subsection{Ethics and artifact handling}
+All new validation uses local static materials and self-constructed benign inputs. The revision does not access real cloud accounts, assume roles, replay bearer credentials, run public workflows, or modify third-party repositories. Required identity tuples are test data, not usable authentication tokens. Public materials are presented as supplied source evidence rather than as reports of newly discovered exploitable vulnerabilities.
+
+The manuscript avoids assigning blame to maintainers or claiming that a selected repository is currently vulnerable. A frozen before-policy may be historical, incomplete, conditional, or unrelated to a current deployment. Repository identifiers are retained only to support the provenance of already supplied cases. Source records and third-party excerpts retain their provenance; inclusion does not create a new blanket license for external material.
+
